      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>CYCLING AND MOTORING.</head>
        <p>CYCLING AND MOTORING.</p>
        <p>CYCLISTS MUST BE PEDESTRIANS.</p>
        <p>I have the following very illuminating letter from a reader on the effort to make an offence of a cyclist wheeling a bicycle without a light:— "I am employed on munitions' work," he writes. "and I use a bicycle to go to and from my work. It saves me more than an hour a day, and that is a consideration when you are as tired as some of us chaps get at the end of a hard day. Last night I was riding home when my lamp went out. I found that the wick had burnt out. Of course, I had to walk home. I interfered with nobody and got in nobody's way. Can you tell me what harm I was doing? If some of them chaps that make laws like this had to work as us fellows do they would be doing a lot more to help on the war." Can anyone tell me what harm the lightless cyclist was doing? I advise my readers to put this case before the local M.P. It is typical. Nobody lets his lamp go out wilfully. The punishment of having to walk home is deterrent enough. But run it in to the local members— for only Parliamentary action will cause a with- drawal of the obnoxious rule. KEEP YOUR CHAIN CLEAN.</p>
        <p>There is no feature of a cycle which plays a more important part in its easy ruuning than the chain. Yet, curiously enough, there is no part more often neglected. It is a messy job, I know, to tinker with the chain, but this is no reason for leaving it severely alone. The modern chain, being a series of rollers sliding over cogs, requires constant lubrication. When oil is withheld the friction set up is obviously very great. Being exposed too, to rain and rand, unless a gear case is fitted, it is also liable to rust. That is why I believe in cleaning and oiling the chain at peri- odical intervals. Every five hundred miles or so, I take off my chain, and withdrawing the pin, zoo&amp; it in paraffin for a night, give it a final scrub with a toothbrush, hang it up to drain, restore it to its cog wheels and give a thorough dressing with graphite, or better still, the special chain lubricant that is supplied by most cycle dealers. The machine, especially up hills, seems to run as</p>
        <p>, 3 SPEED GEAR ..I.lrog 1)t , r S iIjRMEII4IIII 4 FOR CYCLES AND MOTOR CYCLES SVRMEY-ARCHER GEARS LIPNOTTINCitiAM</p>
      </div>
