      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>I 11</head>
        <p>I 11</p>
        <p>easily again after this treatment, reducing one's labour and increasing one's pleasure to a wonder- ful degree.</p>
        <p>HOW TO TIGHTEN LOOSE NUTS.</p>
        <p>To the motor cyclist, be he ever so careful in his monthly overhaul, loose nuts are a constant source of worry. This has been realised by many manufacturers, who recommend all, riders to have a good look over their machines every week, tightening up any nuts that require it. This is a confession on the part of the makers that all is not right with the nuts. But it is rather a tall order to have to try every nut on the machine once a week, especially as some of them are so unget- at-able. The vibration on a motor cycle is enor- mous, and it is not surprising that plated nuts on plated bolts should work loose. Locking or spring washers are not often seen, nor are castellated nuts and split pins often fitted. These may be rather more costly, but one is not bothered by the loss of nuts, on the road, which is a great thing. A good plan, as adopted by one maker, is to use a split spring washer under each nut, and when this has been tightened the bolt is given a tap which has the effect of " burring" the thread, which pre- vents the nut from coming loose. A thin black paint is used on the nut, which renders it vibra- tion proof, THE STORAGE OF PETROL.</p>
        <p>I am quite aware of the fact that the difficulty nowadays is not so much where to store petrol as where to get it. But we are all in the dark regard- ing the new petrol regulations, and as it's pos- sible that we motorists may have to take our supplies when we can get them—possibly several weeks' allowance—it is as well to know what the regulations are concerning the storage of petrol. The owner of any petroleum spirit in a store- house which is situated within 20 feet of (1) any other building, whether or not in his occupation, or (2) any timber stack or any other inflammable goods not owned by him, shall give notice to the local authorities of the district, and shall renew such notice in January each year, and shall permit any duly authorised officer of the local authority to inspect such petroleum spirit at any reasonable time. This resolution does not apply to petroleum spirit kept in a tank forming part of a light locomotive. When a storehouse forms part of or is attached to another building, and the intervening floor or partition is of an unsubstantial or highly inflam- mable character, or has an opening therein, the whole of such building becomes a " storehouse," and may not be used as a dwelling or place where persons assemble. Further, a storehouse shall have a separate entrance from the open air dis- tinct from that of any dwelling or building in which persons assemble.</p>
        <p>SPOKES.</p>
