      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>GLOAT AT HIGH PRICES.</head>
        <p>GLOAT AT HIGH PRICES.</p>
        <p>Trades Council Complain Of Exploitation.</p>
        <p>The Trades and Labour Council are kicking against the high cost of living, and now that the Government have declared that they will not regulate the prices of food the Secretary (Mr. Charles Dukes) reckons that there is no longer a case for wage l not being increased to meet the extra charges. The discussion at the Tracks Council meeting on Tuesday was interesting and sometimes entertaining, and the suggestion was agreed to that the matter should be considered by the branches, and, perhaps, later there might be a. general conference in the town, as others be- sides trade unionists were greatly concerned, and common action might be effective with those in authority. The general opinion was that consumers are being exploited. "Everyone is exploiting us," declared Mr., Blackwood. "If you go into an ice-cream shop, you get half as much as you used to do for a penny. If you buy an eel you have to pay double the price for it, although I suppose they'll say it takes twice as much to rear one. The same if you buy a rabbit in the Market. I don't know whether it costs double as much to rear wild rabbits." (Laughter.) Mr. Hainblett said the other day he over- heard a farmer say to another they were in a terrible stew because potatoes had dropped to 16s. a hamper! The ordinary price was 55., commented Mr. Hamblett. The farmers were not alone in their greed, for sometimes he overheard millers and bakers, and they seemed to gloat over the fact that prices were "what they are." Regarding the necessity for concerted action, the Secretary said that later on the Town Council might be asked to consider the ques- tion, for they also were kreatly affected by increased prices. If they refused they would not be acting in the interests of the ratepayers. Mr. Dykes contended that employers had not set aside antagonism as they said they would when the war began. In the House of Com- mons every private interest in the country had been fought for as tenaciously as though the super-profits of these companies were: pre- requisite to the winning of the war. Search the lists of any company in England and they would be astounded at the amount of profit now being made as compared with the pre-war period. Out of the -t600,000,000 worth of extra production made last year over 1914 .only .245,000,000 of it had been devotid to wages, and that , fact proved that the patriotism of employers generally was mere lip-service, for they talked to the public in one strain and among themselves in another.</p>
        <p>Vicar's Help No Use. William: "Blessed if I ain't bin and cut down the wrong tree, sir. I'm afraid master 'll be mortal annoyed. Sympathetic Vioar : "Never mind, William. I'll tell him it was an accident. Where is he? Up at the farm?" William "No, Sir; 'e be under the tree IN</p>
