      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>POLICE FIELD GLASSES.</head>
        <p>POLICE FIELD GLASSES.</p>
        <p>Eight Men Caught " Napping " At</p>
        <p>Appleton.</p>
        <p>"Gaming is going On Sunday after Sunday, and owing to the seclusion in which the men locate -themselves in the fields we have great difficulty in catching them, so I hope you will take that, in consideration and impose the maximum _penalty of £2 on each defendant, order that the practice will .be put down." So urged Superintendent Owen; at the Daresbury Petty Sessions, held at Stockton Heath. on Tuesday, when he was prosecuting eight Appleton young men, named Harry Brocklehurst, Allan Preston, Alfred Jones, Frank Shuffiebotham, Victor' Miller, William Mowsdale, Arthur Jones and James Lee. All but the first and last-named pleaded guilty.- Acting-Sergeant Worth gave evidence , that, along with P.C. Wellings, he saw through a pair of field glasses all the defendants sitting in a ring in a field adjacent to Green-lane, Appleton. . The police approached gradually and unseen, and behind a hedge six yards away saw all the defendants playing "nap" for money. In fining the defendants £1 each, the chair- man. of the magistrates remarlVd that the justices were perfectly determined to put down gaining. There were plenty of 'games which could be played without gaming, he concluded,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>\l4ll LDER REFUSED CERTIFICATE.</head>
        <p>\l4ll LDER REFUSED CERTIFICATE.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Munitions 'Court on Mon- day, a moulder employed by a Grappenhall Gas Stove Company applied for a certificate. He wished to join the Naval Reserve, but the firm would not let him go. He had been two ycars with the firm, and gave a fortnight's notice, which expired on the preVious Friday. The representative of the company stated that the man was in a certified occupation, and the labour conditions made it necessary for them to apply for War Office badges. The applica- tions had been forwarded, with this man's name on April 28th, and he was asked if he would wait for the reply, but he refused. The man was attested, and was called in his group to report on May 20th. The notice was returned to Colonel Mocatta, and the latter informed that war service badges had been applied for. They were allowed to retain the man pending the decision. The certificate was refused.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>KILLED.</head>
        <p>KILLED.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE H. SUTCLIFFE.</p>
        <p>Missing Since September Last ; Now Reported Dead.</p>
        <p>No further news having come to hand con- cerning Private Harold Sutcliffe, of the local Territorials, who was reported missing on the 25th September last year, the authorities have informed his parents—Mr. and Mrs. T. Sutcliffe, who live at 243, Liverpool-road—that they con- clude he met his death on that date. Private Sutcliffe, who was 19 years of age, and an employe of Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and</p>
      </div>
