      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>PRIVATE J. PENDLETON.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE J. PENDLETON.</p>
        <p>A sergeant in the Border Regiment has writ- ten to Mrs. Pendleton, 17, Scott-street, inform- ing her of the death in action of her son, Private Joshua Pendleton, of the Machine Gun Section of that regiment. The writer speaks in high terms of the great courage and coolness which Private Pendleton displayed "under conditions that were almost sufficient to turn anyone's brain." Private Pendleton, who was 26 years of age, had been in the Army for about seven years. He was previously employed by the Mersey White Lead Company. He was educated at St. Ann's School.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>BOMBARDIER W. J. STOKES.</head>
        <p>BOMBARDIER W. J. STOKES.</p>
        <p>Bombardier W. J. Stokes, of the Royal Field Artillery, whose home address was 13, Lord Nelson-street, is officially reported to have died from cholera on June 22nd. Bombardier Stokes, who was a married man and the father of four young children, enlisted on the 26th August, 1914. He was formerly employed at the Viaduct Works, Earlestown, and later as a furnaceman at the works of the Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Co., Ltd., where he was employed twelve years. A keen footballer, he had played for Garswood and Richmond's Association teams. He was in his 33rd year.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>ago he was exceptionally Cheery and said that the Germans did, not know his number, and it was necessary,' before a bullet or shell hit you, that it should be numbered. He was looking remarkably fit —being 6ft. in height— after about twelve months in the trenches. His brother, P.C. Borrows, of the Borough Police Force, is a corporal in the Grenadier Guards, stationed in London. His brother-in- law, Mr. J. H. Knowles, school attendance officer, was a lanee-corporal in the Territw ials and was discharged as medically unfit for ser- vice after having been wounded in April last year and pending eight months in hospital. S</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>CORPORAL E. J. LLOYD.</head>
        <p>CORPORAL E. J. LLOYD.</p>
        <p>Old Soldier Wounded A Second Time.</p>
        <p>Corporal Lloyd, of the South Lancashire Regiment, and a married man, of 74, Grafton- street was wounded on June 26th for the second time, the last occasion being at Hooge in June of last year. Corporal Lloyd, who is 40 years of age, has served in the Army for many years, having formerly belonged to the King's (Liverpool) Regiment. He served through the South African War and obtained two medals, with five bars. He completed his term on the Reserve a month before the present war, upon the outbreak of</p>
        <p>4</p>
        <p>BOMBARDIER J. W. CHESTER.</p>
        <p>tWounded.</p>
        <p>that he has been brought to an English hos- pital, from where he has written to say he is progressing favourably. Bombardier Chester, who is in his 30th year, enlisted at the outbreak of war and has been at the Front for about three months: A man of fine physique and possessing a good turn of speed, he was a good all-round Player, and was selected to play for Lancashire in a county match in 1913-14. He played for the local team on several occasions when on furlough from his military duties and the last time he met with, a slight accident, neoessitating an exten- sion of his furlough.</p>
