      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>Bridge St., Warrington.</head>
        <p>Bridge St., Warrington.</p>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>THE WELLFIELD STREET TRAGEDY. Judge Tells Jury That There Is No Case.</p>
        <p>PRISONER DISCHARGED.</p>
        <p>William Picken, 183, Wellfield-street, War- rington, surrendered to his bail at the Men- chester Assizes on Wednesday to take his trial on the charge of 'killing his wife, -Catherine' Robina Cunningham Picken, to whiah charge' he pleaded not guilty. Mr. IL Lindon Riley (instructed by the Town Clerk) appeared to prosecute, and Mr. W. Madden (instructed by Mr. T. S. Steel) appeared for the prisoner. Mr. Riley had just risen to open the case for the prosecution, when Mr. Justice Low intervened and stated that he had read the depositions and asked ,31r. Riley whether he thought there was any pose'bility of obtaining a conviction for man- \ slaughter. It appeared to him that there waa considerable Provocation and thati, there waa the probability of an accident. e 'thought 04 Il jury would, not ,return a verdict of guilty , and under these eircumstances.it would' be a waste of time to try the case. Mr. Riley, who expressed some sympathy with his Lordship's expressions, stated that he was entirely in his Lord:ship's hands. The Judge then directed the jury to return a verdict of " Not guilty," and the prisoner was discharged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>SUB-TENANT'S LEAVE-TAKING.</head>
        <p>A SUB-TENANT'S LEAVE-TAKING.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Tuesday, James Hayes, owner of a cutting shin.) in Tilley- street, summoned Samuel Sproston, 13, Norman - street, for doing wilful damage. Complainant said he sub-let a room to defend- ant in his cutting shop for about -a month or six weeks.. He stayed a longer time, and when complainant got work for some men, he told defendant to go. The latter did not like this' and had boasted what he would do to the property. At 9.30 on Friday night the shop was left secure, but the following morning all the wooden brackets in the room were found smashed to atoms.</p>
        <p>Asked if he h,ad witnesses to whom the de- fendant had confessed that he had smashed the property, complainant said he had, but they were not in Court.</p>
        <p>Defendant said that all the property whicb was broken belonged to him. The Chairman of the Bench (Councillor fr• Roberts) said that on the evidence before thou the magistrates could do nothing but dismiss the case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>"INQUIRING THE WAY."</head>
        <p>"INQUIRING THE WAY."</p>
        <p>Having allowed a steam motor and trailer to stand in Manchester-road, and thereby cause an obstruction, Frank Davenport, 17, Seel- street, Stockport, was fined 10s. at the Borough Court on Tuesday. Special Inspector Mason said the steam waggon was drawn across the road at the corner of Manchester-road and Padgate-lane. The engine was drawn up almost level with the door of the Cemetery Inn and the trailer Was acroe9. the road. The defendant came out of the Cemetery Inn, and 'when questioned, said he had been in the public-house to inquire his.wa3r to Liverpool, as he had not been that way be fore. Witness wen, into the public-house and defendant's mate said they knew the journey, having been on the road the day before. Defendant still adhered to his statement that he was a stranger to the road and stopped solely to inquire his way. He drew up his machine in such a position so that he could turn either way. Fined 10s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>DRUNKEN MAN'S GOOD FORTUNE.</head>
        <p>DRUNKEN MAN'S GOOD FORTUNE.</p>
        <p>Found lying in an unconscious condition from, the effects of drink in Stafford-road O' Saturday, Harry Blain" was conveyed to thP Police Station. On looking through his pockets' the police found him possessed of £l9 Os. and he he was much relieved on finding, when be, regained his senses, that the money had g°' into safe hands. At the Borough Police Court on Monday tho Chief Constable said this young man was only' 22 years of age, and when asked what be wanted with so much money in his pocket re- plied that he was saving up for a holidBY- Here he was earning £2 or ..t2 10s. a week at munitions, while some of our men were getting only a shilling a day. The magistrates reduced, the cash in liana by a 10s. fine.</p>
        <p>TANNERY EMPLOYE'S FOOLISH ACT. A charge of stealing a quantity of pigskior the property of Messrs. 'Hutchings, tanners,' was preferred against a middle-aged rao' named William Henry Scott, 13, General-street, at the Borough Police Court on Tuesday. The Chief constable explained that the rnaLl was before the Court on Wednesday of last wee'''. on .a charge of having stolen 61b. of leather ,- valued its., the property of some person tin; known. Detective-Sergeant Summers so' Detective Turner paid a visit to defendant's residence last week, and in a back kitchen found the leather. While there the prisoner Carat) and said, "I know what you have come about. I have worked in a tannery, but I did not take. it from there." Asked as to how the leather came into his possession, he said he got it .in public-house for eighteenpence from a man In did not know,- The got information ths* Scott was soling and heeling boots at t 6 ridiculously low price, and they thought the° was something fishy in the matter. He IL's' remanded for a week for the police to make in;, quiries, but as no owner of the leather hau been found that charge had fallen through. jo The prisoner, however, had also remarked L., the detectives "The only thing I have taken the piece of pigskin." Consequently, he charged with that offence, and in reply said 1110 took the piece of skin which was lying in tD</p>
        <p>yard." For the defence, Mr. A. Browne ksaid prisoner threw himself entirely on the merli of the Court. He had been in Warrington 3 t .is life, and there was not a single comPlain,f against him. ' He had eight children, ono whom was fighting in France. He had 1eefl employed by Messrs': Hutchings for about eigu," s-oars. The piece of pigskin which he admitted taking from the yard had been 1311; about for some time. He took it home for toe purpose of making socks for his clogs. (Mr. Browne) understood that the firm willing to take him back into their emploY• 'Fined 305., or in default, one month's prisonment.</p>
        <p>WHISTLING TO RAISE THE WIND. nO* An elderly man named John. Wilkinson, ott,c,,e settled abode, was charged at theltorough Court on Monday with. begging in Bridge-stree't o:1_ the, previous Saturd,ay.—Detective-Sergel.Tg. Summers stated that he saw defendant Vul''po from shop to shop the *hole night. l',/7,;n-ct searched at the Police Station they money distributed all over him, in every Pec,''' He had ten shillings in silver and 3s. pence and halfpence. Defendant': I was only playit"y, a whistle. be The Chairman said if, he did not leave ,tlt- town another charge would probabltybebr°llls age wainostdd be fined him, and hi. eoswould be sent to H Pris°</p>
        <p>, A MONTH PER PAIR. isll- - months' hard labour was the PlTort.. ment visited upon Mrs. Alice Berry, of 17' ' -01 other shops ljinid-street, by the Borough magistrates,r_.,i Thursday, for stealing three pairs of boot :1,14; Mwic.karYrer's pawnbroking establishment il vy to , -street. The prisoner sent her childrell_t_ gear. . to raise money on the stolen I°'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>lattrs toot Oita</head>
        <p>lattrs toot Oita</p>
        <p>TO A VICTORIOUS END.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The 4th August will mark the ccmcll:- 81on of the second year of the war, and—let us hope—the beginning of its victorious end. The Central Committee for National Patriotic Organisations, of which the Prime Minister is President, and Lord Rosebery and Mr. Arthur Balfour, vice-presidents, proposes that the Meetings and demonstrations held on ' the 4th August last year should be repeated through- out the United Kingdom and the British Empire on the same date this year, and that at each meeting the folloiing resolution should be Moved:—</p>
        <p>"That, on this, the second anniversary of the declaratioa of a righteous war, this meeting of the citizens of - records its inflexible de- termination to continue to a victorious end the struggle in maintenance of those ideals of liberty and justice which are the common and sacred cause of the Allies."</p>
        <p>May I express the hope .that Mayors and Chairmen of Urban and Rural District Coun- cils and of Parish Councils will respond to this -proposal, wherever it is possible, through- out the County of Lancaster, whose brave men have borne a glorious part in this great struggle for freedom and right.</p>
        <p>(Signed) SHIITTLEWORTH, Lord Lieutenant. 13arbon Manor, Kirkby Lonsdale, 10th July, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>RED CROSS SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>RED CROSS SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Proficiency Badges And Certifi-</p>
        <p>cates Presented.</p>
        <p>Owing to lack of space we were unable last week to, publish the names of the successful candidates ul the recent Red Cross examinations for pro- ficiency badges and certificates, and which were -Presented by Mrs. Henry Greenall. at Raddon Court Hospital, before a large gathering, on the 7th inst. , Mies S. Broadbent presided, and those present 4neluded Mrs. Fox, Dr. and Mrs. Murray. Dr. and .1148. G. W. N. Joseph. Dr. Gilder, Mrs. Broadbent, L. J. T. Daman and Mrs. Darwall, Mrs. H. V. Pigot. Mrs. Fletcher (Whitley), Mrs. Bower iStretton). Mrs. Dakin, Mrs. Osborne, and Miss Ynson, secretary. Miss Broadbent drew attention to the increasing 'need for nurses owing to the fact that our armies Were now in motion, and invited all who could Dossibly do so to give their services to Red Cross Work. She also said it was a great satisfaction that Mrs. Henry Greenall had become president of 14,11 e local division of the St. John Ambulance ziriga,de. The list was as follows:</p>
      </div>
