      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>SCHOOL RIVALRY.</head>
        <p>SCHOOL RIVALRY.</p>
        <p>Board Of Guardians On Roman Catholic Schools.</p>
        <p>value of denominational schools were at,ched against those of the Poor Law at Wed- 17v,uaY's meeting of the Board of Guardians, to tra Miss Broadbent reported that she had ic visited the Roman Catholic Schools at and Layfields and was very pleased what she had seen. r• Thomas Dale, another of the deputation, 1371 that at first he was opposed to the children thing transferred from the Cottage Homes to v.° Roman Catholic Schools, but he could now :tilt that the children were under the best ton- all seemed to be hapety, and were well- , v:rhe Rev. A. M. Mitchell expressed the wish wlatt C.hurch of England children could be dealt in in the same manner. It was a better 48_,rin than the one they had at the Cottage th"les, although there was every reason to be ,ankful for the care bestowed on them there. All'he Chairman (Mr. A. J. Jolley) maintained 1:1-att there were no better schools than the best- i_aliaged Poor Law Schools, and they as Poor Guardians had no reason to be ashamed. 141188 Broadbent: As far as others than theu'a,n. Catholics are concerned, I do not think eee Icould be better than they are in our 00s. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>etburrn</head>
        <p>etburrn</p>
        <p>WAR INEFFECTIVE</p>
        <p>_</p>
        <p>• • •</p>
        <p>looks best. Oddments of ribbons make blouses, bags, hats and many other trifles. Sets this year are almost a craze. It started in America, and is finding favour here, because .given taste, and some originality, with skill in manipulat- ing odds and ends, the most charming details of dress result. A patterned ribbon and some raffia straw can be converted into a charming hat for the seaside, the set being completed by a bag, big enough to accommodate more than one kind of useful work, such as knitted scarves, or the more dainty underwear that so many women are making themselves in these war times.</p>
        <p>Puddings Without Eggs. Omnibus Pudding.—Take floz. flour, floz. fresh suet shred fine, 6oz. raisins stoned, 4oz. golden syrup, a teacupful of milk; mix well, put into a basin, tie a cloth over and boil for two or three hours.</p>
        <p>Treacle Pudding,-11b. flour, 31b. suet chopped very fine, lb. treacle, pint milk, loz. ginger, half-teaspoonful soda; put into a basin or mould and steam for two hours and a half.</p>
        <p>Paradise Pudding.—Cut some bread and butter as thin as possible, line a plain shape or basin with it; boil up any fresh fruit with sugar, such as apples, damsons, 84e. (remove the stones); when quite boiling pour some of the fruit into the basin, then another layer of bread and butter, and so on in alternate layers until the basin is full, taking 'care that the last layer is of bread and butter; let it stand until quite cold, then turn it out on to a dish; a little juice reserved from the boiled fruit may be served in the dish by way of sauce, and powdered sugar should be sprinkled over the pudding.</p>
        <p>Excessive Use of Salt and Sugar. In cheese dishes there is generally enough salt in the cheese without adding more. One of the most remarkable habits of our time is the excessive use of salt and sugar. Not only must we teach the children to do with much less sugar, but we must learn to do with much less salt. We add it to nearly all our savoury vegetable food ,in the cooking, and over and above that, we add still more at the time of eating. No dinner table is considered complete without one or more salt cellars; some people take as much as three-quarters of an ounce or even loz. a day. Now the question is not, of</p>
        <p>course, whether salt is necessary or not, but whether there is a sufficient quantity already existing in our food. Some people think there is a great difference between added salt and that natural to raw foods; tfiat the former (the added salt) is inorganic and almost impossible to assimilate, and is even poisonous, while the natural salt is organic and nutritive. It is only those who have discontinued the use of added salt for a time, who know how delicious vege- tables and other foods can be without it. Even those vegetables that were thought to be almost tasteless unless seasoned are found to have very distinct flavours. People mostly urge that condiments are necessary to stimulate diges- tion, but, on the contrary, they will be found to cause much over-eating. If food cannot be eaten without them it is generally a sign that there is something wrong with the digestive system, and so it is better to abstain from food until a natural and healthy appetite is felt. An excess of salt means a great deal more work for the kidneys in separating it from the blood prior to its expulsion. Indeed, it is sometimes thought that some cases of inflammation of the kidneys originate in the excessive use of salt, and what has certainly been proved is that patients suffering from this illness very soon improve if they are placed on a dietary free from added salt.</p>
        <p>Evelyn-street Council School. 870 Sarah Ashton, 44, Selby-street • age 13; Evelyn-street Council School. 871 Amy Moral, 13, Fox-street; age 13; Evelyn-street Council School. 872 Nellie Lee,L, 68, Lovely-lane; age 12; Evelyn-street Council School. 873 Edith Carter, 174, Wellfield-strect; age 13; Evelyn-street Council School. 874 Dorothy Cotton, 117, Liverpool-road, age 12: Evelyn-street Council School. 875 Edna Cotton, 117, Liverpool-road; age 9; Evelyn-street Council School. 876 Annie Whitley, 37, Mill-lane; age 13; Evelyn-street Council School. 877 Lorna Reading, 27, St. Barnabas'-place; age 13; Evelyn-street Council School. 878 Olga Bruhl, 24, St. Barnabas'-place; age 11; Evelyn-street Council School. 879 Edith Morris, 61, Priestley-street; age 10; Evelyn-street Council School. 880 Muriel Sleigh, 5, Lovely-lane;. age 12; Evelyn-street Council School. 881 Eva Layton, 3, Hepherd-street; age 12; Evelyn-street Council School. 882 Amy Lowe, 18; Monk's-street: age 11; Evelyn-street Council School. 883 Annie. O'Neil, 3, Selby-street; age 12; Evelyn-street Council School. 884 Ellen Deakin, 331, Liverpool-road; age 10; Evelyn-street Council School. 885 'lda Overend, 34, Mill-lane; age 11; Evelyn-street Council School. 886 Doris Barlow, 93, Wellfield-stceet; age 11; Evelyn-street Council School. 887 Doris Gregory, 9, Mill-lane; age 12; Evelyn-street Council School. 888 Lizzie Lewis, 21, Samuel-street; age 13; Evelyn-street Council School. 889 Rosie Walker, 97, Bramhall-strent; age 9; Evelyn-street Council School. 890 Alice Henshaw, 99, Bramhall-street; age 11; Sacred Heart. • 891 Hetty Eccleston, 4, Bramhall-street; age 12; Evelyn-street Council School. 892 Marion Fellows, 18, Pickmere-streetv:age 12; Arpley-street School. 893 Maud Alice Lunny, 108 a, Bridge-street; age 10; People's College School. 894 Evelyn Mason, 8, Frederick-street, Latch- ford; age 12; Bolton Council School. 895 Annie Thomas, 8, Warrington View, off Wash-lane, Latchford; age 12; Bolton Counc