      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>To Turn The Thoughts Of People To Religion.</head>
        <p>To Turn The Thoughts Of People To Religion.</p>
        <p>The Rev. T. T. James preached at Wycliffe Church last Sunday night on "The Church and the Churchless Masses," the fourth of his series of addresses on "The Church and the War." An Old Problem.</p>
        <p>The problem of the churchless masses, Said the reverend gentleman, was an old one, which he would staten a sentence. We were confronted by vast masses of the community who are out- side the bounds of the Christian Church. There were very many who imagined and hoped that one of the compensations of the present conflict would be found in a return to the Church and to its worship of those who had been long absent, a reawakened interest in the message and mission of the Church. The tremendous realities in the midst of which we lied to-day were exp:ctal t) bang a restoration of interest and contact between the Church sat". those separated from her. To the best of his know- ledge nothing of the sort had, taken place. Any signs there were of this change at the beginning of the war had almost entirely disappeared. Atrophy of the Soul.</p>
        <p>"We ought to realise," he stated, "that while there are those whose religious convictions take a deeper note by reason of the experiences through which we are passing, there are also those who feel this war to be a treniendons challenge to Christianity, for whom the whole fabric of the Christian faith appears to totter and reel in face of the turmoil and tumult of physical combat, for whom the very exietence of God seems to become a doubtful thing in the presence of this catastrophe. But Ido not think we misread the signs of the times if we say that the number of those separated from</p>
        <p>.• the Church by mental and spiritual uncertainty or even by a definite and ordered spirit of antagonism, is relatively small compared with the number of those who remain outside from the spirit of religious indifference. One primary problem is with those who are suffering from atrophy of the soul, and who are quite content to live their lives apart from God. The exigen- cies of the present moment, the blood of battle- fields, and the grim uncertainties of national destiny, and the agony of sacrifice and suffering, have not re-awakened in them the desire for the deepest things. That is the problem of the Church. _ _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>Positive Message.</head>
        <p>A Positive Message.</p>
        <p>"We shall not readily or quickly find a solu- tion, but there are two oi three things which the Church of Christ may well ponder in face of the gravity of this outlook. We must, as a Christian Church, have the positive note in our message. Doubtless a restatement of the Christian faith is necessitated by the findings of modern knowledge, but the Church must have the note of certainty about the things which lie at the centre of the Gospel. We shall not meet uncertainty outside the Church, and we shall not combat it by uncertainty within. We need to remind ourselves again of the simplest and yet most difficult of all truths: the greatest argument for Christianity is still a Christian life, and the greatest recommenda- tion of the Church is in the moral purity and goodness of, Christian ,men and women. We need to recover the evangelical spirit. I be- lieve with all my heart that if the piurch is to maintain its place when the war is over it will be necessary for us to take up the work of, evangelisation. 'The Son of Man is come to seek and to save that which was lost.' The possession of this Spirit will give a new devo- tion to our worship, a new homeliness and warmth to our Church atmosphere, a new im- pulse to our prayer, and a new ingenuity to our methods."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>HONOURING THE DEAD.</head>
        <p>HONOURING THE DEAD.</p>
        <p>Communion Cups &amp; Trays Presented At Golborne Street Baptist.</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening, at Golborne-street Bap- tist Church, a very interesting service was held, when a good congregation assembled to honour the memory of some of those who in times past were connected with the church as members.</p>
        <p>The occasion was the presentation and dedi- cation of individual Communion cups and trays, given to the church in memory of the late Sergeant-Major T. S. Davis and the late Mr. and Mrs. A. Few.</p>
        <p>The service was conducted by the Pastor (the Rev. H. Ogden), who preached from the text: "Apart from the shedding of blood there is no remission." The preacher mentioned that some years ago Sir W. Robertson Nicoll com- menced a sermon in the pages of "The British Weekly" by heading his subject "Without the shedding of blood. . . ." Without the shedding of blood—nothing; and that is true in all ranks of human life. Unless a grain of wheat fall into the ground it abideth alone. Even the humble potato sacrifices its own being to create the new cluster around its root. That was equally true in human life. No man or woman could have, the joys of fatherhood or motherhood without the shed- ding of blood. A mother especially risked her life; hence the tenderer feelings of a mother. The late Mr. and Mrs. A. Few gave of their best in giving their son, Mr. B. Few, who, through all the sunshine and shadow of the church life, had been true to the church. Words were not adequate to describe the sacri- fices of the late Sergeant-Major Davis. When at home he gave his time, money and life for the young men of the church and for the mem- bers of the Boys' Brigade. He died in France, giving a brave life for his country. Though passed into the Better Land, he still spoke to us.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>Cups Presented.</head>
        <p>The Cups Presented.</p>
        <p>The cups and tray in memory of Mr. and Mrs. A. Few were presented to the church by Mr. B. Few, their son and senior deacon of the church. They were presented on behalf of Mrs. Few and himself. He said that it was the joy of his life thug to honour, the memory of his parents and urged the young people of the church to follow their piety. The cups and tray in memory of Sergeant- Major Davis were presented to- the church, on behalf of Mrs. Davis and the late Sergeant- Major's mother, by Mt. Norman Balmford, of Manchester, for long associated with Golborne- street as deacon and church treasurer. As a personal friend of Sergeant-Major Davis and the family he declared it was one of the privi- leges of his life to have known Sergeant-Major Davis intimately. He was a man who was straight, honest, true and self-sacrificing, and no more fitting memorial than Communion cups could perpetuate his memory. The trays are of oak, to match the Com- munion furniture, and suitably inscribed. Two solos, "He shall feed His flock" and God, to Thee," were tastefully "Nearer,.ende ray rendered by Miss capel. Mr. S. Rickers pre- sided at the organ. A solemn Communion was well attended at the close of the service. ,attended</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>REV. 11. T. DIXON'S PROSPECTIVE</head>
        <p>REV. 11. T. DIXON'S PROSPECTIVE</p>
        <p>SUCCESSOR.</p>
        <p>If the Conference approves, the Rev. Preidk. Hudson, who lies been a missionary in the Transvaal for eight years, will succeed the Rev. H. T. Dixon as pastor of the Penketh Wesleyan Church in September. Mr. Hudson is a son of the late Rev. William Hudson, a brother of the Rev. Dr. Hudson, and has the reputation of being a gifted minister.</p>
      </div>
