      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJO4IE.</p>
        <p>The Welfare of the Young.</p>
        <p>To deliver 'a Priestley lecture is indeed an honour, and that this distinction fell to the Duchess of Marlborough is the due reward of merit for patriotic service. The Duchess took for her subject "The Saving of the Children," in which she has been deeply interested ever since she made her home in this country. Her Grace has for a long time cherished the desire of establishing in London a National Institute for Mothercraft, and the war has furnished the opportunity of realising her idea, which has the support of many other women inter- ested in the preservation and betterment of infant life. The Duchess announced that plans are in preparation for such an institution, which is to be a lasting and fitting memorial to those who have given their lives in battle to save British women and children. The Power of Pence.</p>
        <p>Children have been as zealous as their elders in working for war funds, but the result of the efforts of Jeannie Jackson, the twelve-year-old daughter of a Burnley miner, constitutes a record. The child's brother was killed at Loos, and she conceived the apparently impossible idea of collecting enough money in the streets and factories of her native town to pay for a motor ambulance to be sent to the Front as a memorial of him. For over a year the child gathered pennies, mostly from the workpeople, and was at last enabled to realise her ambi- tion. When the pence amounted to the hand- some sum of £1,200 an ambulance complete in every detail was purchased. This was taken to Marlborough House for Queen Alexandra's inspection, and, at Her Majesty's special re- quest, the child and her mother accompanied it. The motor is called "The Young Kitchener." Present Fashions.</p>
        <p>There is always a dearth of new modes during the month of July, which is by common consent given over to the sales. The new and enlarged silhouette has so far made but ‘little headway; perhaps women are afraid that the crinoline lurks behind it, and hesitate to adopt it for that reason. The short skirt has certainly gained in length by some inches since the warmer weather made the wearing of shoes a necessity. With this kind of footwear the very short skirt is impossible, although with high boots it is fascinating on tjie young and slim— but on them only. Among decorative effects that generally come in at the end of the sum- mer season lacing deserves special notice. It is seen on blouses, jerseys, and jersey gowns; in the latter case it often extends from the opening in front to the knees. Sometimes the lacings open at the top to show a dainty guimpe. Ribbons, too, are being used in a number of ways unthought of a couple of years ,ago.</p>
        <p>The Vogue for Sets. For instance, a modest length of a very wide brocade or chequered ribbon makes a delight- ful coatee for wearing over a transparent blouse, and it may have short sleeves, a basque, collar, and a band. Narrower widths, that have picot edges, can be used to widen skirts in the form of stripes going from waist to hem. If the material of the skirt is flowered, the ribbon should be plain; in any case, however plain, faille, satin or taffetas</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
