        <p>Conducted by UNCLE JACK. 4 .:.</p>
        <p>4. 0. 4. •4. .:. 4. .:. .:. .:, 4. .:. 4. .:. .:. 0 .c. ie. .:. 4.404 4</p>
        <p>NEARLY 1,000 NEPHEWS AND NIECES.</p>
        <p>My Dear Children,— A fortnight ago I said I should like to make my family of nephois and nieces 1,000 'strong before the holidays, and you have helped me very well indeed. As you will see by our list this week, we now number 923, which is only seventy-seven short of the total 'wanted before the schools close. Perhaps you will get me these " recruits " by next week? You will remember that promised a prize for the two boys or girls who sent in the most names for membership this week. ' The first prize goes to 693 Niece Kathleen Smith,</p>
        <p>43, Mill-lane, who attends Evelyn-street Council School, and is. One of my best corre- spondents. The second prize (equal to the first prize) is won by _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>827 Florence Seabury,</head>
        <p>827 Florence Seabury,</p>
        <p>3, Frederick-street, Latchford, who attemls Bolton Council School, and was next to the prize winners last week. Among others who did very well in securing new members were: Nephew Harry Heywood, 8, Matthews-street; Niece Mabel Wright, 68, Winwick-street; Niece Dorothy Wright, 68, Winwick-street; Niece Edna Healey, 851 Hoyle. street; and Nephew Horace Himsworth, 15, Fitzherbert-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>DRAWING COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>DRAWING COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>We will have another drawing competition this week. Draw anything you like, and send it in any time up to next Wednesday morning. Don't colour the drawing. Your holidays begin next Friday, and I sup- pose you will be looking forward anxiously to them. If the war prevents your parents from joining in this year as previously don't mind. All those who can work must work now. We can, and will, work the Germans off the battle- field. Only work can do it, so let us all resolve to help all we can. Willingness to forego pleasures will help considerably, and next year we may be free to resume our holidays just "as usual."</p>
        <p>Your loving,</p>
      </div>
