      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>PAY IN LIEU OF NOTICE.</head>
        <p>PAY IN LIEU OF NOTICE.</p>
        <p>A striker, formerly employed by a Warring- ton firm of iron and brass founders, claimed compensation for dismissal without notice and in lieu of pntice.</p>
        <p>Mr. Charles Dukes represented the trade society in support of the man's claim.</p>
        <p>ApPlicant's- ease WaiTihat•—;;;irs;l7tTillness he could not go to work on June 25th, and he was told subsequently that his services were not Wanted as a striker. He was asked to give up his badge. His wages were 295. weekly. It transpired that a leaving certificate was given bearing date 25th June, but the firm's representative explained that this was a clerical error.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dukes told the Chairman that the man had dace got employment, and could have been working that day if he had not had to attend the Court.</p>
        <p>The representative said the man was told his position as striker was filled, and that they could offer him a position as labourer.</p>
        <p>3I r. Dukes: You can't reduce a man's status under the Act.</p>
        <p>The Court awarded 14s. 6d. compensation.</p>
        <p>=MN</p>
        <p>_ . _ - • - .</p>
