      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, JULY 15, 1916,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>"GOT STRAFE ENGLANDWE CANNOT."</head>
        <p>"GOT STRAFE ENGLAND- WE CANNOT."</p>
        <p>'As we go to press, at the end of the secondl week of the great Allied Offensive on the Western Front, news' comes that all is still' going well with our arms. On Tuesday, SIR DOUGLAS HAIG was able to report. that the gallant forces under his command had cleared out, bag and baggage, the first line. of the enemy's defence over a continuous stretch of eight miles. Last night's messages were to the effect that he had followed up his thrust and had, in an additionitl three days, broken into the enemy's seqond line of defence for a distance of four miles.</p>
        <p>In the transition stage between the taking of the first and the attack upon the second line, there have been sanguinary conflicts for the possession of two strongly fortified woods —the Mametz Wood and the Trories Wood. The importance of these' two positions in the scheme_ of defence of the enemy is best gauged by the frantic efforts and the tragic losses he has been willing to make in order to regain and to retain them. He did partly regain them, but he has failed to retain them. Bazentin le Grand is well beyond the former wood, and Longueval is quite a mile beyond the latter wood—and those villages are among the places that fell into our possession yesterday. So that Trones Wood with its slaughtered Prussian Guards may be looked upon as a memory now by the Hun. This sustained fighting for a fortnight With its forward motion unabated, 'is symboEc of the whole situation. Our contemptible little Army has grown into one ,of exceedingly large dimensions; our works at home have supplied the munitions commensurate with the size of' the Army; and now we are on the offensive—as the Hun is finding out to his cost. Our wen are there; their comrades-in- arms who are working, at home will be I represented by the guns and shells they send out. The men who have left the trenches will ask no rest till they have chased the last German across the Rhine; the men in the workshops will, we know, abandon• their holidays willingly so that the eilemy may be given no rest. We are at a most critical period of the war. What we have done is quite apart from what the French have done on our immediate right. Pressure applied here and now by the Armies of England and France may bring about such complications in the opposing military machine that its collapse will have been brought appreciably nearer. Clearly the German cry "Got Strafe -Eng- land" is becoming rapidly more necessary to the needs of the Fatherland, for it in certain that dermans will never be able to strafe us.</p>
        <p>While we are rejoicing over the glories of the triumph of a righteous cause we shall not forget the price we are paying for it. Our columns to-day, bear sad testimony to the i ravages the war is making n our homes and our family lives. Grief that is calculated to crush the life out of loving hearts has been thrust wholesale and callously upon us, and we fear much more sorrow will be caused before the end is reached. , But those who suffer through our noble dead, or the maim- ing of those they hold most dear, will have a double consolation. They know that those who have gone down ,have given their lives for their country willingly, and those left behind will know what has been done by their kittoh and kin has saved their successors from a thraldom besides which death' and suffering are of little account.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>WAR SAWNGS WEEK.</head>
        <p>WAR SAWNGS WEEK.</p>
        <p>Next Week's Effort To Boom The Scheme.</p>
        <p>Next week there will be national effort. to boom the scheme for saving by the people in order that the Government may be free of the expensive and cumbersome and, in some way, undesirable, method of raising the money by formal loan. There is already a War Savings Committee in Warrington,: and with it ate affiliated the following associations; formed to save by means of certificates te..be bought by regular contributions, which will grow in value under a system already.' explained in • "The Examiner ":—Warrington Secondary School, Warrington litinicipal..officers,.Working. Men's Mission, Fairfield School, Co-operative Sociekv, Ou4trington, Sandy-lane School; Silver-street Senior Bible Classes; Helsby Council</p>
        <p>A public meeting is to be held 'at the Town Halt on Tuesday, to which the representatives of the workers of all the firms in town are in- vited, with a view :to the formation or these savings associations in every works:. Repre- sent's:fives of other bodies and schools are in- vited.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>To Meet Financial Strain.</head>
        <p>To Meet Financial Strain.</p>
        <p>Vittory will rest with , the side that -can londest bear the financial strain; and 4whiles'our boys at the Front ,are giving themselves, those of us at home cannot forbear the little self- sacrifice that will be necessary to continue find- ing the money to support them. We can all do something at any rate towards pe(rforming our duty by becoming a member of a War Saving Association. It is an easy way of saving money at a big rate ef interest.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. L. Venables, the hon. secretary of the committee, Market Gate Chambers, will be glad to give any information that may be desired.</p>
        <p>Flag Day Next Saturday. A: Flag Day in aid of. the hospital funds of France, Russia and 'ltaly will take place in Warrington and district next Saturday. The event has only to be mentioned to be appreciated in all its bearings—social and financial.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 15, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>jib,„ iNGToN e NEL, •</head>
        <p>jib,„ iNGToN e NEL, •</p>
        <p>ith the promotion of Major Gordon Roberts to the rank of Lieutenant-Colonel, Warrington now local Territorials, and four of them are natives of the town, Colonel Layton being the exception. Our</p>
        <p>LIEUT.-COL. J. DUTTON FAIRCLOUGH, Son of Mr. John Fairclough. Commanding Second-4th Battalion, Sputh Lancashire Regiment.</p>
        <p>.LIEITT.-COL. A. B. LAYTON. Commanding Third-4th Battalion, South _ . .</p>
        <p>Lancashire Regiment.</p>
