        <p>LIEUT.-COL. e. GORDON ROBERTS, Son of Councillor Henry Roberts. Ccnimanding a Battalion of the Lancashire Fusiliers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>HOUSES NEEDED!</head>
        <p>HOUSES NEEDED!</p>
        <p>But Taken Down For Works'</p>
        <p>Extensions.</p>
        <p>SERIOUS OUTLOOK.</p>
        <p>Responsibility Of Firms And Of</p>
        <p>Town CoUncil.</p>
        <p>At a time when the scarcity of houses has become an urgent problem, the proposed demolition of twenty-five dwellings .in order that, extensions may be embarked on by one of the largest firms in the borough, prompted members of the Trades and Labour Council to protest strongly at their monthly meeting on Tuesday evening, and to call their Executive' Committee together _next :week to- consider ways and means for dealing with the situation. As the Secretary (Mr. Charles Dukes) said, there is a Munitions Workers' Health Committee, and by that time information will have been gleaned as to •vitat powers they possess for action. The question was introduced in a general w by the Secretary, who said he had received communications from other Trades Councils asking them to consider the housing problem, as it was becoming a most pressing one, and urging them to suggest the provision of house by the municipal 'authorities. Mr. F. Berry thought that at the present time no governing bodies .would start building houses. A. Member: It is a very grave situation. lam in a fix as I Jive, under (mentioning the name of the firm referred to). Tenants of theirs have received a month or two's notice, and I don't know what we am going to do unless they turn us out into the street, °for we cannot g 4 anywhere else to live. There are not any other houses to be obtained. .</p>
        <p>Mr. Shimmi4 : Could we do anything in the matter—make a protest? The Tenant: They were approached to build a row of houses, but they pOoh-poolied, the idea., Another Member: They want a Toon profitable speculation. Another Firm "Guilty."</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Mr. Edwin Plinston) : All we can do yet is to sympathise with these people. Any protest should come from the tenants themselves. Mr. H. Harrison: Another firth is guilty of the same offence. Couldn't they be stopped making these extenSions? They are doing it to avoid paying the Excess Profits Tax. The Secretary: To be quite frank I don't see what you can do. It is a contract between the owners of the houses—the firm—and the ten- ants, and it is just as permissible for them to request you to leave as it is for you to hand in the key. It is private property—that is the evil. That is one of the things we should per- haps boast about as being a liberty. (Laugh- ter.) The only solution of the problem is for the Corporation to build houses. That we have always regarded as being their function, but I don't suppose there is one on the Town Council who thinks the same I am of opinion that the housing problem is one of the gravest issues to be faced. The necessity of the firm is that they desire to extend their plant, and, after all, you cannot interfere, for it is private property and they have a perfect right to do it. Unless you can get the' Corporation to build houses there is no solution whatever. Mr. Shimmins thought that they as trade unionists and ratepayers ought to approach the Town Council and make a protest. Town Councillors Decried.</p>
        <p>Mr. Berry: At present it is futile to approach the •Town Council, for the members are there in combined effort to increase the competition for houses and not to relieve the national diffi- culty—and I' don't suppose they would admit _there is a national necessity. Mr. Shimmins : We could go, and if they don't act we could show them up. Mr. Berry 'thought that if the firm concerned wrote to the Town Council they would have con- siderable weight. , The Tenant suggested that they should leave the firm out of it as far as any protest went, but they might approach the local authorities to try to persuade the company to build houses for those to whom • they had given notice. As far as the firm was concerned the Trades Coun- cil could not do anything: they had got to take it sitting down. Mr. Shimrains: I don't. think the Town Coun- cil is _so rotten that they will not kick against it. I think 'We have some men on the Council who will act as they should do. The Secretary thought that instead of passing any resolution now they should call a meeting of the executive next week, by which tine they would probably have more information. There was a ,Munition Workers' Health Committee, which might have a voice in the matter, but he was not sure of what their powers were, and would have to make inquiries.. . _</p>
        <p>.• _ This suggestion was acquiesced in as stated above.</p>
        <p>MESSRS. PEARSON &amp; KNOX% LES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Requested To Extend In The National</head>
