ly as possible." fieance and importance of music in public won- Sunday School Teachers' "Quiet Day?' ship and pointed out the prominent place A "Quiet Day" for Sunday school teachers in which the choir held in the services of the preparation for the National Mission of Repent- Church. . The choir, which has reached a ance and Hope, was held at the Parish Church high state' -of efficiency, under the organist, on Saturday. There were a series of devotional Mr. E. Davies, sang the anthems "Come, Holy services from three to eight o'clock,_and several Ghost" (Attwood), and "0 taste and. see" quiet intervals were spent either in the church. '(Goss). or the Rectory grounds. Three impressive Mr. and Mrs. George Pilling addresses on Sunday schc;ol work, its importance Congratulated. and the mental fitness necessary in the teachers Mr. and Mrs. George Pillirig,‘ of Oak Bank, were given by, the Venerable Archdeacon How- Latchford Without, having recently celebrated son, who mentioned that a special committee e had been formed in the Liverpool diocese to deal their golden wedding, they wer congratulated by the Runcorn Board of • Guardians and the with the children's aspEct of the National Runcorn Rural District Council ti; their meet- Mission. Tea was served in Fairfield Schools in . . 1 ngs -at Dutton Workhouse, on Tuesday, Mr. , the afternoon, and the day closed with evensong 1 . . • Pilling being a member. Mr. Woodward, who 'in the church. This event took the place of the -- proposed the, resolution, said Mr. Pilling had Igarden party usually given in the summer by , . . been a highly-respected Member for many years, ' the Sunday School Teachers' Association. I and they wished him and his wife long life and much happiness. In seconding, Mr. C. E. Linfaker, sen„ remarked that looking at Mr. Pilling's juvenile appearance one would not have thought he had been married 25 yeas, never Mind ' fifty. Mr. Pilling was one, of die most regular and ,useful members. Severab membars associated themselves with the' compliment, and ,Mr.. Pilling suitably replied.</p>
        <p>HOUSES NEEDED !—Continued.</p>
        <p>the case should be stated ,exactly as it stands</p>
        <p>_ "At the request of the Minister of Munitions, a form of invitation which in the case of a ' Controlled ' firm differs little, if at all, from a command, we have arranged and are proceeding as rapidly as possible with extensive additions co our Wire Mill Plant to make good the danger- ous shortage of wire rods, due to the failure of German supplies-, and which cannot be secured in any other way, and are urgently required for the national defence. To make room for these alterations we have been compelled to appropriate every yard of land available in our Bewsey Works and to Tiff-Chase some adjoining property in its place. It is a necessary part of these alterations that the .25 cottages in Bewsey- road and Price-street should be Oiled down and, so as to inconvenience the 'tenants as little as possible, they were at once informed, what our plans were and advised to look out for other accommodation. We shall Certainly delay this part of the scheme as long as we can, but it is impossible to do so indefinitely. "The question of better housing accommoda- tion is urgent and pressing, and the effect of recent legislation has been to discourage private enterprise in the building trade, and as a result a real famine in house property is already upon us in Warrington, and the welfare of the town must grievously suffer 'unless the question is dealt with forthwith. Then? seems to be Ti() alternative but Municipal acttion, and if the Corporation have not already powers sufficient to enable them to take the matter up and supply the urgent wants of the town, no time should be lost in obtaining these.—,Yours faithfully, for the company, JNO. J. BLECiLY.</p>
        <p>Musical guccesses.</p>
        <p>,At the recent examinations held by ,the Trinity College of Music, Miss Ada Benn3tt, pupil of Miss J. Farrar, has successfully pas3yl the "first steps" grade. The following pupils of Miss M. Jennings, 142, Wilderspool-causeway, passed very successfully at the recent examination in connect i.Oll with the Victoria College of Music, namely: —Miss Ellen Miller, 12, Nora-street, piano diploma (Licentiate), 94 marks, and Miss Hilda Baker, The Park, Penketh, 92 marks out of a possible 100, entitling them to append the letters L.V.C.M. after their names, also to wear the College Cap, college shape, red tassell, Gown the same as associate, but with a two inch edg- ing pf red silk to sleeves. Hood, royal blue corded silk, lined with red silk. Miss Alice Furnival, 53, Bewsey-street, piano diploma of Associate, 82 marks, which- entitles her to append the letters A.V.C.M. after—E'er name, also to wear the College Cap, Gown and Hood. Advanced Senior Grade: Evelyn Fraser, 63, Gaskell-street, Stockton Heath, 80 marks, Honours. Senior Grade : Mary Hart: 4, Eustace- street, 80 marks, Honours, and Arthur Harrison, 73, London-road, Stockton Heath, 78 marks, let class. Junior Grade: Hilda Mary Dyke, 190, The Causeway, 89 marks, Honours. primary Grade, Doris Glover, 3, Fairclough's avenna, 97 "marks, Fronotris, and Evelyn "Pr —erringtoll, "36, Tanners-lane, 90 marks, Honours,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>Wood's College.</head>
        <p>Wood's College.</p>
        <p>• Wood's College, which occupies the first and second front suites of chambers over the Lon- don City and Midland Bank, 15, Bridge-street, has issued a prospectus explaining the courses, the terms and the advantages, present and prospective, of the institution. If students are prepared to work there is no fear of disappoint- ment, says the Principal, either as to the re- sult of the lessons or the positions found for those qualiry to fill them.</p>
        <p>Piling It Up. Admiral Sir A. Mostyn Field, acting conserva- tor, in his report for 1915, states that in the length of river between Bank .Quay, Warring- ton and Rowley Weir, 318,869 tons of material have been diedged from the channel and have been deposited on land above the influence of the highest tides. The Admiral adds ,that in the length of the Manchester Ship Canal be- tween Eastham Locks ,and Latchford Locks 751,970 tons of silt have been raised by the dredgers. The total quantity of silt dredged from the canal during the year was 2,441,737 tons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>Scholarship fcpr St. Barnabas' School Boy.</head>
        <p>Scholarship fcpr St. Barnabas' School Boy.</p>
        <p>- After An examination recently held at the Manchester Grammar School, the Governors have awarded a Foundation Scholarship, ten- able for ag long as he shall remain at the v^hool, to Henry Neville, Hollingworth, son of the Rev. J. IL G. Hollingworth, vicar of St. Ilarnabas'. Master Ilollingworth has been a holar of both the infants' and mixed depart- ments of St. Barnabas' Day School, and is now nearly 1l years of age and in Standard VI.</p>
        <p>Secondary .School Scholarships The following is a list, arranged in order of merit, of the candidates who have been suc- cessful in gaining junior se,holaiships tenable at the Warrington Secondary School :,--Annie Dusekworth, 'Albert .Greenough, John N. Pad- more, Denis Jones, Ada Freeth, Harry Johnson, Harry • Lee, Alice M. Sankey, Vernon Vavasor, [John Edgar, Elizabeth M. Knight, Ethel Mel- j-Nmrne], [Cyril G. H. Bayliss, Eileen Morton], [John P. Shaw, Ethel A. Baird], Gwendoline L. Humphreys, Lucy LygO, Evan Evans, Sidney Woodward, .Emily Sutton, Arthur Hale; Ruth Parsons, Frances C. Wright,. James Woodhead, Archie George. Those in brackets are equal in merit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Raddon Court Pound Day and Entertainment.</head>
        <p>Raddon Court Pound Day and Entertainment.</p>
        <p>The monthly pound day and entertainment in connection with the Raddon Court Military Hospital took place on Thuisday afternoon. the ustlal exhibition of contributed goods in the hall, the party retired to an ante- room, where an interesting vocal entertainmeat was provided. The concert was presided over by Miss S. Broadbent The prizes for the best piece of work executed during the past month by the soldiers was decided by a vote taken by the inmates of the hospital, and recorded in favour of 1, Private. Goddard (a fancy cushion); 2, Lance-Corp ral Scott (fancy embroideu woyk). After the entertainment the party assembled in the hall and partook of afternoon tea, which was daintily served by members of the staff. The value of the goods received was £3olls.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Roman Catholic or Protestant?</head>
        <p>Roman Catholic or Protestant?</p>
        <p>On the initiative of Mr. W. C. Brooks, the Board of Guardians, on Wednesday, decided to submit certain facts to the Local Government Board, he stating that "I a waster of a woman," who is a Protestant and whose husband (who is a Roman Catholic) 'had had to leave her owing to her habits, had entered the Workhouse with her four children and had their religion given as members of the Church of England, instead of Roman Catholic; the religion of their father. He knew for a fact that the childr..n were Roman Catholics. Mr. W. Flanagan mentioned that Father Whittle had christened two of them. The Chairman (Mr. A. J. Jolley) having explained that they themselves were powerless to alter the entry as the mother had called them Protestants,, the Board agreed that the facts €hould be forwarded .to the Local Government Board and ask them to allow the record to be revised.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>PR!SONERS-OF-WAR.</head>
        <p>PR!SONERS-OF-WAR.</p>
        <p>Do Not Forget The South Lancashire Lads.</p>
        <p>Everyone must be aware of the scandalous manner in which the majority of our men who I are prisoners-of-war in Germany are being treated as regards the necessaries of life, and a very earnest appeal. is being made to the public on behalf of the lads of the South Lan- cashire Regiment, whose welfare is of such moment to Warrington and district. We feel sure it has only to be made known that the Mayor's Fund is, feeling a strain just at pre- sent to. have a generous response, so that the cpst of providing the men with comforts (about £35 per week) will be regularly maintained. Parcels of food and other pressing require- ments to the value of ss. 6d. each are regularly dispatched to every man, and, as Warrington is the \depot, town of the regiment, it must not fail in keeping up the gupply. Every day at the Parr Hall letters and cards are received from the various camps where our men are interned, expressing appreciation of the effort being made in Warrington to maintain the men in something approaching living conditions. As an illustration, a letter received by Mr. and Yrs. A. Gibson, 5, Mill-street, from Private W. Pratt, now a prisoner at Cellelager, Prov. Hannover, Deutschland, says that he is doing well, but finishes up with a ," request for a parcel," Private Pratt, who belongs to the Middlesex Regiment, was the first to send authentic information of the death of Mr. Gibson's son at Hooge a year ago. For a period, owing probably to money being required for other local needs, the subscription list has naturally shrunk, and it is hoped that as the result of present efforts it will regain its former position. Subscriptions may be for- warded to the Mayor at the Town Hall, or to the Borough Treasurer. Anyone interested in the welfare of Our prisoners-of-war or wishing to know, about the dispatch of food-stuffs and other comforts will , get every information at the Parr Hall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>LIELTT.7COL: BRERETON</head>
        <p>LIELTT.7COL: BRERETON</p>
        <p>Son of Mr. R. T. Fairclough. Commanding First-4th Battalion, South Lancashire Regiment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>These who could not understand why the workers were asked to give up their Whitsun holidays because the need of. munitions was urgent and were promised a longer holiday in August in compensation were many.</p>
        <p>The reason for the postponement has been seen during the, past fortnight, the goods sup- plied halving lifted the Hun across the equiva- lent of a county in the neighbourhood of the Somme. \</p>
        <p>But it looks as though continued work would be as necessary in August as it was at Whitsun. The Government have suggested the further postponement of holidays for reasons which need no stating.</p>
        <p>How much is a sovereign worth? This absurd question was answered in the House of Commons on Tuesday, when the country was informed that its purchasing power (as compared_ with March, 1914), is 15s.'</p>
        <p>Strange that golden sovereigns, should do down, while say twopenny pies are selling at twopence halfpenny</p>
        <p>Mr. Francis J. Coakley, assistant master at St. .Mary's School, a time-expired Territorial, who has seen much active service, has been recalled for further service. The, Education Committee have granted the necessary leave and arranged for the asual allowances.</p>
        <p>"Up to date over 400 trade union offiCials have been exempted, and we can say we .have taken a lead .in getting that done,". remarked the Chairman (Mr. Edwin Plinston) at the Trades Council meeting on Tuesday, after the Secretary .(Mr. Chas. Dukes) had mentioned that in reply to the circular letter they had sent to various Trades Councils regarding the exemption of trade union officials from service, twenty had supported them and only one had been against.</p>
        <p>"Is Mr. Dukes one of the 400?" I asked the Chairman after he had pronounced the Bene- diction—" Good night, friends !"—and the reply was, "Ng. not yet. His case has not yet been dealt with."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>DUMB SPEAK.</head>
        <p>THE DUMB SPEAK.</p>
        <p>Two Patients Surprise Their</p>
        <p>Comrades At Winwick.</p>
        <p>Two cases of soldiers recovering their speech after being rendered dumb through shock in the great advance have occurred at the Lord Derby Military Hospital,, this week. The return of this power came with surprising suddenness under very interesting circumstances. Corporal Charville, of the 2nd West Yorks Regiment, is 'one of these lucky fellows. He was dumb for ten days after being blown into the air by a mine explosion. He was leading a bombing party in the attack on La Boiselle, and When they had reached the enemy first-line trench he sent four of his men down to explore the deep dug-outs. The explosion then occurred, and he was blown about 30ft.</p>
        <p>On Monday night the corporal was in bed writing a letter at the 'hospital. It appears that he had to borrow a pencil. The man in the next bed to him was ad,eep, so that he could not ask him for the loan Of his, which he could see in his locker. Being a soldier, he quietly commandeered it. After he had finished he was returning the pencil when the man who owned the locker opened his eyes. The corporal, sur- prised, said, I've only had the loan of your pencil." These were his first words for ten days. As soon as they heard the corporal speak some of the felloWs.got out of bed and u-ent and con- gratulated him. There was general rejoicing in the ward, and the sensation was made an excuse for the patients to be allowed to talk an hour longer than usual. The corporal took his turn with barrack-room' gusto. It was a great night for him.</p>
        <p>The other case is ,that of Private Sam Hep- worth, of the K.0.Y.L.1., who ,has been in the hospital since the 7th inst. His dumbness was caused by shell shock, and on Monday he was reading in:bed.when he suddenly looked up and exclaimed to the 'comrade in the next cot, "I can speak." His words proved it. Both. of them now vocalise with the fluency Which become soldiers, and instead of spending their afternoons in.hed have been enjoying the recreation afforded the other convalescents.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>– KNOCKALOE TRAGEDY.</head>
        <p>- KNOCKALOE TRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>Williams Committed For Trial.</p>
        <p>At the Criminal Court, Peel, yesterday, Sergeant John Williams was charged with murdering Colour-Sergeant William Henry MaHugs by shooting him. The evidence was a repetition. of that at the previous hearings. The Deemster, in addressing the jury, said the in- quiry was as to whether there wits sufficient evidence to send the prisoner for trial on the charge of murder. The jury, without retiring, found there was. Prisoner, who was legally represented, reserved his defence. The date of the final trial has not yet been fixed. Prisoner appeared in better health than when formerly charged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>RUMOUR RE GEORGE THOMAS.</head>
        <p>A RUMOUR RE GEORGE THOMAS.</p>
        <p>A rumour was in circulation yesterday that George Thomas, the well-known Warrington Northern- Union footballer, had been killed in the great advance. Our representative inquired from his brother-in-law, but up to the time of going to press no official notification had been received. The report gained currency 'through a letter from another Warrington soldier to a friend in Warrington to the effect -that Thomas had been killed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>RAIDING THE ENEMY TRENCHES.</head>
        <p>RAIDING THE ENEMY TRENCHES.</p>
        <p>Warrington Officer Leads Successful Assault.</p>
        <p>INFLICTS TERRIBLE EXECUTION</p>
        <p>IN DUG-OUTS.</p>
        <p>As the War Office has already made clear, the British Forces in France have recently made many successful raids upon the enelnY trenches, and have in the process not only inflicted heavy losses upon the Germans, but gained valuable infortnation as to the state _of the opposing lines at the present 'critical juncture. A vivid description of one of these raids ig furnished by a lieutenant of the Inniskillings, who, when war broke out, was a teacher at the Beamont Council School.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>RAID LIKE A REHEARSAL</head>
        <p>RAID LIKE A REHEARSAL</p>
        <p>Writing to his father, a well-known Sankey, resident, the gallant officer says : " Well, here I am safely back again and In billets after our raid, which was a great suc- cess. We had a terrible experience, but we have the satisfaction of knowing, on the other hand, that we did a terrible amount of execu- tion. The circumstances of the whole affair were most peculiar. ot My parties were going to their positions on our own trench when a very heavy bombardment was opened all along our front line. This was fifteen minutes before we were due to leave. How- ever, we pushed on to the rendezvous, and, taking the opportunities as they came, I pushed ,my parties through the barrage and out to a sunken road that runs parallel to our lines and half-way across to the Bbche. We all got into position without a casualty: the meantime our guns were silent, as they had their programme prearranged, and it was impossible to alter this to cope with the fresh developments. At the time appointed they opened with a roar over our heads as we 10' there. First the ten-pounders and then the 4.5 Howitzers all pounded away at 'yritz's° front line. The 60-pounders and 8-inch Howitzers got on to his second line and all went hard at it until, at a prearranged time, the guns on the front line immediately in. front of us switched to the right and left, and in we went. The men were splendid and went over the open exactly as though at a rehearsal. My wire-cutters sent back word that the guns had cut completely through the wire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Bombs For Boches.</head>
        <p>Bombs For Boches.</p>
        <p>" The parties jumped into the trench and got to work. On the left we were checked almost immediately, and my blocking partY of four 'men were heavily bombed, one being killed and two wounded. Their flanking part'''. rushed after them, and a quick interchange of bombs took place. The Boche was driven back about twenty yards, and left a dug-out, from which the occupants tried to shoot as my men turned the corner. The leader of the flanking party and two men were hit, but the rest rushed the dug-out and about fifteen bombe soon accounted for that lot. We now ran TIP against a second Boche bombing party, which was in a special bombing sap, and they gave, us a warm time, so I withdrew the party ana, got them up under the parapet. The right-hana parties were doing their job well, so I gave the left •instructions to hold on there and bloFk</p>
        <p>the trench: which they did. cii;.--tite-Tighi the first party found a nest of dug-outs and 'Fritz' just beginning to come up. The leader, 3 magnificent sergeant, just dashed along, shoot- ing anyone he met, while his three men threw 'bombs down the dug-outs as they passed and then_ carried on to the point which they had</p>
        <p>to - block. The flanking -party followed .imroe• diately after them and got to work at tbe dug-outs. They shouted for the Boche to come up, but they were very gain° ' and fired up at our men, so the only thing was to kill 'em—too risky to go down, as the dug-onts were crowded with men, even the steps being full. My fellows simply stood round the corner of each door and threw bombs as fast as they could. • Six dug-outs alone got 150 bombs, and the cries and moans were terrible.</p>
        <p>A Dangerous Return.</p>
        <p>"Rifle grenades were now corning over frail _ . _</p>
        <p>the support trenches, so after twelve minutes I decided the only thing to do was to get out, so out we got, and as each party reported an out they sneaked :back to the sunken road, young Stevenson and I coming in, last and bringing with us a bridge, which had been, taken up for crossing the trenches if ;neces .al - sary. We l got back to the `road, . carrying our casualties with us. We didn't try to get back to the trench, as the barrage ,was too hot and we hoped to lie low until it was over. Five mihutes after we got there the Boa° began to barrage the road with mortars, high explosive and shrapnel, and for an hour and a half we lay out under this hail of fire.. The men stuck it like Trojans and moved quietli up and down as directed to avoid the hottest places. This part was the worst of the Whole proceeding, and I had 26 men hit and one' more killed. How we escaped at all is miracle. I was twice half-buried by big" explosive shells dropping on the bank in fronl and behind. A shell dropped only four fee; away from another officer and myself an" failed to explode. McKinley, however, we,°' hit in the thigh shortly afterwards, but To; badly. We all wore our steel helmets atiti, special steel body shields, which saved lets 0; further casualties. ;After an hour and a half of it the bombardment ceased and we got back with our casualties, which now amounted t° two killed and 24 wounded. I carried in a sergeant who was rather badly hit, and theri got a party from the trench and carried hint up to . the advanced dressing station. ' The doctor was having a 'fearfully 'busy time, 60 T got to work and helped as far as I could. There were some terrible sights, I can assure you. _</p>
        <p>Heavy Enemy Casualties. "I have just come back from a visit to the casualty clearing station, where I drove over with the Colonel this afternoon. It was a pit ful sight to see those fine fellows, many g' whom will never fight again. They were the pick of the regiment, too, but that always is t.11.,,e case. I see in to-day's `Continental Dam' Mail' the report of the raid appears under the heading of Raids by British Trocl* Altogether at a moderate estimate ts must have killed at least 60 of the enemy—proably double the number, as the dug-outs we packed. We found a machine gun emplace,' ment, but it had a steel door, so a corporal eil,“ I got out and over the parapet to the loopluge and threw bombs through there. At any ratei the gun never opened again, so something rags have happened: "Altogether the whole thing was a gr- slice*, and we've had congratulatory message from all quarters. I'v been put next for leave' so hope to get home very soon now."' The writer of the above has since taken Paret in 'the advance for which he helped to PrePae the way, and has, we are pleased to say, CO ,through, so far, untouched.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>TOBACCO, PLEASE!</head>
        <p>TOBACCO, PLEASE!</p>
        <p>Appeal For Our Heroes Frorl The Western Front. as The Lord Derby Hospital at Winwick Al, been suddenly filled by new men, who ally have few friends at hand, and, corair straight from the Front in a wounded cool tion, possess but few comforts themselves- t 9 fortnight ago the hospital had only 400 Patieviler within its spacious halls; to-day it has ev'Ar, 2,000, or within 200 of its full capacity. tunately the majority of the cases are —0- serious, and the men are quite capable a e a iaYing a pipe or a cigarette and the great,fies. sudden inrush has overtaxed available 61113k' nd The public are therefore requested to sea, 0 P Pipes, tobacco, cigarettes, or money toto chase these aids to comfort, with all speed THE ADMINISTRATOR, LORD DERBY WAR SOSPITAI" WINWICK, oee ho will be happy to receive theni and t° them placed to the best advantage.</p>
        <p>AUXILIARY HOSPITALS. ml'ated There are 1,200 beds in the hospitals am-1 the With this great war hospital, including ,ese T rmary, Whitecross and the Barracks. Ti?,_i 1 , av also received 'contingents of woundedir of i Week, and doubtless the management a them wciuld welcome' some kindly consid t ailii,t the the lines we have suggPsted in respe 'of era.t u the Lort-Derby Hospital. ,</p>
        <p>t</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>IN THE 2IDVIINeE.</head>
        <p>IN THE 2IDVIINeE.</p>
        <p>Many Warringtonians in the List of</p>
        <p>KILLED.</p>
        <p>Casualties.</p>
        <p>BOMBR. J. F. MASSEY.</p>
        <p>Killed By Shell Which Fell Into • The Gun Pit. Two letters have reached Mr. and Mrs. J. F. , 'Massey, 25, Cumbtriand-street, .announcing the death in action of their son, Bombardier Joseph Frede(rick Massey, of the Rbyal Field Artillery: The first letter, dated *July sth, was from Sergeant C. E. Cunuington, who wrote, as follows "It is with deepest sympathy and regret that I write to inform you that your dear son Fred has found his eternal rest. I can hardly ex- Press on paper what a shock it was for us all in the battery when the nelys became known. We rniSS him very much, for he was very popurar amongst us, and I miss him more so because we Were personal friends and often exchanged con- fidences, I know his loss will be a great one to ' You, but you have the consolation of knowing that he fell with his face to the foe and died doing his duty for his King and country. He was firing his gun on the morn- ing of the third instant, and ha doing so for some time, when at 6.30 a.m. an enemy shell burst in the mouth of the gun pit, killing him and his partner and severely injuring another. During the day he lay close to the gun he had selived- so well, and at sunset was reverently laid to rest with his pal in 'a grave which we andd for them close to the battery. They 'ow lie at rest in a grave upon which we have • placed flowers and a cross with their names - and numbers and battery marked upon it. I will turf it if I can get the chance, .and put White stones around it. Please accept through Me-the deepest sympathy of the whole battery, and mine personally, in your great loss, and try tu'be brave in the thought that our Saviour once said "Greater love bath no man than this, that a Mall lay down his life for his friends."</p>
        <p>.d heon</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>Marked for Promotion.</head>
        <p>Marked for Promotion.</p>
        <p>Captain T. McLeod wrote under date June Ath:—</p>
        <p>His death was instant and qvuite painless. A Church of England minister performed the funeral ceremony in the presence of the rest _of the battery. I-feel the loss most deeply, as held your son in the deepest respect as one .of my best men, and would have chosen him 401' promotion if he had lived. Please accept MY deepest sympathy in your bereavement." Bombardier Massey, who was 25 years of age, -uad been at the Front 12 months, having joined file Army a month after the outbreak of war. /Jo was formerly employed in the telegraph</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>PRIVATE S. S. SHAW.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE S. S. SHAW.</p>
        <p>The Second Son To Be Killed In Action.</p>
        <p>A second son. of Mr.. and Mrs. George Shaw, of 5, Farrell-street, off Howley-lane, has fallen in 'action. At Easter, Private George Shaw, of the Wiltshire Regiment, was officially reported to have met his death after having been reported missing for twelve mOnths, and yesterday morning the news was broken by a neighbour to Mr. and Mrs. Shaw that their second son, Private Samuel Stanley Shaw, of the K. 0.5.8., had been killed. The neighbour had received a letter from a soldier friend, who asked her to tell Mrs. Shaw that Samuel was killed in- stantaneously by an aerial dart. The writer added that Private Shaw would be greatly. missed by all his comrades. Private Shaw, who was 20 years oil age, was of a particularly cheerful disposition, and had made quite a host of friends. He enlisted a month after the outbreak of war, and had been at the Front twelve months. He acted as a signaller. He was formely employed as a wire- drawer by Messrs. Rylands Bros. Ltd., and was an old boy of the National School.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>CAPTAIN C. 'R. CHAMBERS.</p>
        <p>Son Of Poslxnaster Severely Wounded</p>
        <p>In Shoulder.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. Chambers, postmaster, of Warrington, has received 'news that his son, Captain Cyril Roy. Chambers, of the South Lancashire Regi- ment, has been severely wounded. The following telegram from the War Office conveyed the news :—" Regret to inform you that Captain C.cR. Chambers, South Lancashire Regiment, was admitted. to Red Cross Hospital, July 10th, with gun-shot wound in right shoulder. Severe." Details, however, are lack- ing. _</p>
        <p>Captain Chambers, who was promoted in February from Lieutenant to Captain, has been serving since the commencement of hostilities. Twenty-three years of age, he was, prior to war, on the staff of Parr's Bank. He joined as Private the King's (Liverpool) Regiment, latterly being transferred to the Bth Battalion So"iith Lancashire Regiment, in which he gained a commission.</p>
        <p>Whilst in the Army he passed through the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <p>• GUNNER HAROLD GLOVER. Killed in Action. best_o_ bee l'-ed gunner in the battery, and as he has with me since his enlistment, and had 10."11 quite dear to us, the news came as a d a.t 4.ne blow to us all. He will be buried to-night to all the battery will be there to do honour °ur gallant dead. We have had a nice, neat os, made to Ark his resting-place, and I can rislire You it will be well kept." `. , frZunner Glover, who was 20 years 4:lf age, was 8.-LnlerlY employed by Messrs. A. W. Baker and tr". wire gauge manufacturers, Winwick-road. ti-lesowaS educated at St. Ann's Day School and eia_s attended. the Sunday School and Bible 15, Another brother, Frank, is a sergeant Lae A.B.e.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>I'IIiVATE FLETCHER WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>I'IIiVATE FLETCHER WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>Jri'ate Pletcher, of the South Lancashire i'vgiment, whose home is in Battersby-lane, '4B b eell wounded in the great advance, lie is , D. resent in a hospital at Leeds. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>teams. Of one of these he was captain for about two seasons.'</head>
        <p>teams. Of one of these he was captain for about two seasons.'</p>
        <p>SERGEANT S. WHITFIELD.</p>
        <p>AVolinded In Leg And Shoulder With</p>
        <p>Shrapnel.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Samuel Whitfield, of the Manchester Regiment, a married man, of 3; Mersey Mount, Mersey-street, has been vounded with shrapnel in flu) shoulder and leg, find is at present in w Base Hospital. Sergeant Whitfield, who is 30 years of age, was formerly in the employ of the Warrington Tanning Company. He enlisted immediately upon the outbreak of war and has been at the Front twelve months. He was educated at the National School, Church-street. His brother, James, of the South Wales Borderers, was killed in action some time ago; while another brother—John—hs 111 Frame with' the 8.04 ,</p>
        <p>Wounded.</p>
        <p>of Staffordshire, but has been in Warrington about seven years. He has four children. • '</p>
        <p>SERGEANT J. OVERSTALL.</p>
        <p>A Reservist And Former Railway Employe.</p>
        <p>'Sergeant John Overstall, a reservist, of the Lancashire Fusiliers, ,whose home address is 23, Nicholson-street, has received two bullet' wounds, in the upper part of his arm and has been brought to a hospital at Bristol. This is the second time he has been wounded, the first time being on July 7th last year. Sergeant Overstall, who is 38 years of age, served with the colours for eight years and Oft the reserve for a like period. In civil life he is an assistant foreman on the London and North-Western Railway.'</p>
