        <p>CORPORAL H. RUSTIWORTH Wounded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <p>enlisted soon after the outbreak of war,- and has been at the Front eight months. *He was for- merly employed in the drawing office at Messrs. Pearson and, Knowles' Works. He is an all- round sportsman, being a member of the War- rington Cricket and Bowling Club; the Stock- ton Heath HockeY Club, and the Warrington Cycling Club. He, also belongs to the War- rington Musical Society, and has taken an active interest in the local Shakespeare -Com- memoration. He, is an old boy of the Arpley- street School.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>CORPORAL THOMAS MASSE'V.</head>
        <p>CORPORAL THOMAS MASSE'V.</p>
        <p>Wounded By Shrapnel In Arm And</p>
        <p>Shoulder.</p>
        <p>Having come safely thkough the fighting at Gallipoli, including the *first Anzac landing, Corporal Thomas Massey, of the Australian Contingent, and eldest son of Mr. and Mrs. A. W. Massey, 63, Allen-street, Warrington, has been woubded in France by shrapnel, in the left forearm and right shoulder. He is now an inmate of the County of London War Hospital, Epsom. _ _ .</p>
        <p>-Corporil Massey, who was born in Warring- ton, went to Australia seven years ago, where he did much travelling,. and also in South America. He enlisted at 'Adelaide. He was educated at St. Albb.n's School, and before leav- ing Warrington was employed at the Post Office.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>PRIVATE N. ISHERWOOD.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE N. ISHERWOOD.</p>
        <p>'tl ound In The Neck "Nothing To Trouble About."</p>
        <p>Writing from a clearing casualty station in France, Private Nathan Ishrwood, of the South Lancashire Regiment, informed his wife, who lives at. 22, Lincoln-street, that he had been slightly wounded in the neck. "It is very stiff and sore," added the writer, "but nothing to trouble about."</p>
        <p>Private Isherwood, who is 28 years of age, !las been in the Army almost two years and has been at the Front since February. He was 'formerly employed at 7.l.lessrs. Rylands Bros., Ltd., and later, at the Gas Works.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>PRIVATE J. WRENCH.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE J. WRENCH.</p>
        <p>Wounded In"rhe Thigli And Foot By</p>
        <p>Bomb.</p>
        <p>Private James Wrench, who enlisted in the Loyal North Lancashire Regiment immediately on the outbreak of war and has just been wounded for the second time, was employed by Messrs. Thomas Whittle and Sons, Ltd., belt manufacturers, Rose and Crown-street. He is the son of Mr. Thomas Henry Wrench, 38, Gee-street, Dallam-lane, a Pearson and Knowles' employe, now serving with the South Larfeashires (National Reserve) in Wales. Twelve months ago he was wounded at Silvia Bay, in the Dardanelles, and spent some time in Cheltenham and Whitecross Hospitals. Since being wounded at the beginning of the present advance Private Wrench has been in a Bir- mingham Hospital. The nurses of the Chel- tenham Hospital Shaving heard that he is again wounded have sent him a splendid box of flowers to Birmingham. He is an. .old National School boy and was 21 last April. A Terrible Night.</p>
        <p>Writing to his mother, Private J. Wreich says :—" On the night of June '3oth we were all ready for the Germans. We knew it was coming off. We got ready ammunition, bombs and rations. At nine o'clock we blew four mines up. Then the order came 'Get. ready,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
