::,•,,,,•-:,,,,i*:,., :,..,,":„...-•::::•:::,.:::1.-t, ..::.:.,,:,..,..;,.1••;:::.0..:,......,,..‹.-.:;•_::k.:.....,:i-,„•:„.,:..-...:......:.:„..A..:••••....:..-..,:,.::..:.............„. ::::,c,J.,IP,E-....:§•:::;.-4iiii.:3,1,1',4%t;1:P.:.'...,.:1.!:'..r...:57..:'..:.:::::!:1.1.4:1!'54:',."'qii ,:::::::::Iiilliai?..:!!...1..":1.111:::::,-iiiiiimiliMit:11.!..,;:•:,:,.:i:::,;..140,11_0 ..1.E.i:::;.1.1i.iit.1E.:;::::,:;:ft.-imt0!;1•904•R:•,,:::i•t:::.,....6„,'.:,4V . % ...A.,?.4...„,......::,.;•:?....5..:!::,.....:::0•„,:,.,......,..„4.1•1•-g,::!iyitiiii-..-,..,:r:5..,,,,,,...:6,.:•:;:::%•:,:-- .• ......-</p>
        <p>PRIVATE JAMES WRENCH. Wounded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <p>boys.? and overi we went with the best of luck. I hung on the • German barbed wire for five minutes , and then I got into the German trench and it was a fight with fixed bayonets. We came off lucky and took a go'od many prisoners. We fought like rats. We were bombing the Germans for five hours, until a shower of bombs came and knocked us out. That was how I got hit. I crawled out of the German trench under very heavy shell fire and got into a crater. Here I dressed my wounds and crawled to our lilies, risking my life,. but it was a good job I did or I would have been taken prisoner. I got into a dug- out and was there six hours and could not stir for the shelling. Then I risked it again and got down to the dressing station. It was ter- rible that night :.one big light 'kith, the guns constantly firing. There was a terrible slaughter, there being piles of German dead— dug-outs full. I have got two holes in my thigh and one in my right foot."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <p>and I was just entering it when a lump of shrapnel hit me on the head and gave me a gentle 'move on' into the bottom of the trench. It Ives comforting to feel my head still on and I found a big hole in my steel helmet and the rubber concussion cap pierced, and I got k cut .ear. When the reserve brigade came up I pushed on and was Mighty pleased to find our boys secure in a redoubt beyond, and the trenches strewn with dead and dying Bavarians. The counter-attack came on Sunday morning and they tried to bomb the boys out; hut, no! the whole assault was repulsed after heavy losses on their side. Things began to, settle down a little and I was then sent to the rear."</p>
        <p>Private A. Cooper Horobin is 26 years of age and was educate.d at the People's College, .where he gained a three years' open scholarship at the 'Boteler Gramliar School. He entered the service of Parr's Bank, Ltd., being, appointed to their Widnes Branch, where he remained for about five years. He was promoted to Parr's Bank, Crewe, where he remained until joining the colours in September, 1914. He was a member of the Arpley Tennis Club, Warring- ton, and has been a playing member of several tennis, hockey and football clubs in Warrington, Widnes and Crewe. He was one of the active members of the "Old Boys' " Grammar School Football Club. His father, Mr. A. R. Horobin, a prominent Warringtonian, died about twelve months ago.</p>
