, . , ~,,,Z• ~, ... -,....i.U:,;:.:,...... .::.' 'f'47:,, .. . • ~.,U;;:,?.... ~ . . .</p>
        <p>PRIVATE A. C. HOROBIN. Wounded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>RIFLEMAN CECIL COLTER.</head>
        <p>RIFLEMAN CECIL COLTER.</p>
        <p>Says Shrapnel Wound Is_Only A " Bit</p>
        <p>.01 A Scratch."</p>
        <p>• Two of the three soldier-sons of Mr. and Mrs. William Colter, 178, Battersby-lane, have given their lives for their country, and the third, Rifleman Cecil Colter, of the Rifle ,Brigade,. has been wounded. In announcing the news of his mishap in a letter dated July Bth, Rifleman Cecil said he had been hit in the arm with a small piece of shrapnel, "but," he Added, "it is only A. bit of a scratch. We passed a batch of Hun prisoners as we were coming out of the trenches, and they were a poor lot—bits of 'kids' and old men."</p>
        <p>Rifleman Colter, who is 21 years of age, en- listed in September, 1914, having been pre- viously employed as a wiredrawer at the Long- ford Wire Works. He has been at the Front twelve months and has been serving as a first- class signaller.' He was educated at Hamilton- street School and Beamont Council School. The two brothers who have been killed are Rifleman Victor Colter, of the Rifle Brigade, who met his death in October, and Bombardier' Sydney Colter, of the/R.F.A., who died from wounds in hospital on the 28th of last month. The father also is 'un old soldier, and was on the permanent staff of the South Lancashire Regi- ment at Orford Barracks for 21 years. He saw service in the Zulu Rebellion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>PRIVATE RALPH ' FELLOWS.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE RALPH ' FELLOWS.</p>
        <p>Buried Alive In January And Now Receives Two Wounds.</p>
        <p>Buried alive in Sanuary, and ma with diffi- culty rescued, Private Ralph Fellows (21), son of Mr. and Mrs. C. S. Fellows, 18, Pickmere- street, received two wounds when advancing on Friday of last week with the Royal Fusiliers. Receiving a gun-shot wound in the right shoulder, he crawled for thkee hours, saw a dis- tressed wounded comrade and bandaged his face, and while nestling in a shell hole was struck in the left leg with shrapnel. He now in a hospital near Blackburn and by now will probably have been operatd upon. Private Fellows used to work in the labora- tory at Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., worshipped at the Working Men's Mission, and held a lieutenancy, in the Free church Boys' Brigade.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <p>PRIVATE G. C. CLARKE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>Bullet Goes Thtough His Leg With° Touching Bone.</head>
        <p>Bullet Goes Thtough His Leg With° Touching Bone.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Hannah Claike, a widow, 12, Helsby- street, has received news from her son, Private George Clifford Clarke, machine gun corporal, to the effect that he has been wounded and is in hospital in Kent. Writing to his mother, he says:—" As soon as our lade got to the •Germans they dropped their arras. They can only fight by cowardice, and when they come man to man they sur- render. Fancy ten Prussian 'Guards surrender- ing to of% of our men r The bombers were knocking them down like sheep. There were thousand a of shells flying about. The bullet</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <p>PRIVATE GEORGE C. CLARKE.</p>
        <p>Wounded</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <p>RIFLEMAN HARRY NURSE.</p>
        <p>Wounded</p>
        <p>struck his right hand as he was advancing on July Ist. The first time Rifleman Nurse was wounded was in December, when his left side was rather severely hurt and he had to remain in hospital eight or nine weeks. It is characteristic that he should make light of his injuries, for even on that occasion he wrote declaring that his Wound was "only a slight one and you need not worry." Rifleman Nurse, w,ho is 25 years of age, was educated at Silver-street School and was for- merly employed by Messrs. Joseph Crosfleld and Sons, Ltd. He enlisted in Febrnary of last year, and was drafted to the Front in the following June. He is a noted baritone singer and has used his art to advantage in diverse places—churches, concert rooms and music- hallsi With his soldier "pals" he is a great favourite when any concerts are being given. Both his brothers are in the same regiment as himself.</p>
      </div>
