      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>PIifVATE J. HEESQM, D.C.M.</head>
        <p>PIifVATE J. HEESQM, D.C.M.</p>
        <p>Youthful Hero Suffering From Shell</p>
        <p>Shock.</p>
        <p>Private Joseph Beesom, of the Royal. Welsh Fusiliers, who was awarded the D.C.M. in May for valuable services rendered while taking part in a bombing raid, has been admitted to a Liverpool Hospital suffering from shell shock., According to a letter which he sent to his parents, Mr. and Mrs. J. Heesom, 31, Orford- avenue, he was buried in the debris caused by a bursting shell, and had to be dug out. He was visited in hospital this week by his brother, who found him quite cheerful and progressing favourably. Private Heesom, who is only 18 years of age, was formerly employed by the Longford Wire</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <p>Company, and later by Messrs. Bylands Bros. He enlisted in November, 1914. He attended the Brick-street Mission Sunday School, and played -football for the Beamont United Asso- ciation team. Another brother, Henry, serving in the local TeiritoKials, was wounded in June.</p>
