      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>PRIVATE S. EMERY.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE S. EMERY.</p>
        <p>Wounded In The Back "%Vint Machine Gun Bullet. Mrs. Emery, 3, Dalton-bank, has received news from a nurse at a Brighton Hospital that her son, Private Stephen Emery, of the Royal Welsh Fusiliers, has been admitted into the hospital suffering from a machine gun bullet</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <p>Wounded</p>
        <p>that he found a. photograph of her and her children on the stretcher upon which Private McClelland had evidently been carried, and would forward it. '</p>
        <p>Private McClelland, who is 27 years of age and a native of Scotland, comes of a fighting family. His father is an old soldier, and his sons—seven in number—have all seen service.. One, unfortunately, has been killed, while, in addition to Private Leslie, four are at present serting in the Army, and one in the Navy. Private Leslie, who is the youngest of the sons, enlisted on the 26th August, 1914, and was first wounded after being out at the Front for six months. He was granted a sick leave and had been back at the Front since Febru- ary. He was formerly employed as a cooper by Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, but left for Scotland just befcre the outbreak of war to better his position. He has worked in War- rington for ten years and is well known and respected around the Bank Quay district.</p>
        <p>T . :„.. . „.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE LESLIE McCLELLAND</p>
      </div>
