      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>WESLEYA N</head>
        <p>WESLEYA N</p>
        <p>ANNIVERSARY.</p>
        <p>Interesting Services Held Last Week-End.</p>
        <p>Special hymns, solos" and recitations by the scholars, who had been carefully trained by Mr. J. H. Brazendale, was a feature of the 113th anniversary of the Lymm Wesleyan Sunday School, held on Sunday. At the children's ser- vice in the afternoon, purses and aonations collected by scholars and friends in aid of the Sunday school funds, were received by Mr. Frank C. Arney, who delivered an interesting and encouraging address to the scholars, as also did the Rev. A. J. Morton. The collections were in aid of Sunday school work. Services were also ~.iconducted morning and evening, the preacher being the Rev.- ,A. J. Morton, of Padgate. .</p>
        <p>The celebrations were cdntinued in the church on Monday evening, when a grand organ, vocal music and elocutionary recital was given. The programme opened with an organ solo by Mr. Stanley Jones, "United Service," and later in the evening the same gentleman gave a second solo, "A Better Land." Two quartets, "0 Gladsome Light" and "Thou Did'st Leave Thy Lowly Dwelling" were given in a very com- mendable manner by Mrs. S. Knowles, Miss A. Gibson, and Messrs. H. Ruddock and R. S. Stewart. Miss Alice Gibson was heard to advantage in her rendering of the songs "Light in Darkness" and "Sombre Woods." Mrs. S. Knowles very creditably gave the songs, "The Procession" and "The Lost Chord." Miss A. McHugh delighted with her renderingS of "The Task" and "Nearer, My God to Thee," whilst along with Mrs. Knowles and Messrs. H. Rud- dock and R. S. Stewart she took part -In a quartet entitled "God is a Spirit." Miss A. Armstrong, a girl of about 16 years; recited with great effect "The Legend Beautiful" and "Lady Clare." Mr. H. Ruddock contributed in a very creditable manner "Lead, Kindly Light," and `1 Arm, Arm Ye Brave," and ,Mr. R. S. Stewart gave "Be Thou Faithful Unto Death" and "A Soft Summer Breeze." The programme was brought to a conclusion by the singing of a quartet "The Long Day Closes" and the National Anthem.</p>
        <p>In the kingdom of self many a man is his own worst traitor_</p>
