      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 15, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 15, 1916.</p>
        <p>ley (sisters); Mrs. J. W. Davies and Mrs. T. W. Davies (sisters-in-law), Mr. William Davies (Heatley), Messrs. C. S. D. Davies and S. D. Rutter (nephews), Miss V. Davies (niece), Mr. and Mrs. G. Allen (Agden), Mr. S. G. Ware- ham (Dunham), Mrs. J. Wareham (Lymm), Mr. T. Perrin (Altrincham), Mr. and Mrs. Charles Perrin (Altrincham), Miss Timperley, Mr. and Mrs. A. Wareham (Dunham), Mr. J. Perrin (Bollington), Mr. and Mrs. J. Wareham (Dunham), Mrs. Barnett and family (Altrinc- ham), and. Private J. Summers. The Post Office was represented by Messrs. H. Robinson/A. Sutton, S. D. Moston, S. Appleton and T. Knowles; Warburton bell- ringers by Messrs. F. Leather, T. Leather, W. Owen, J. Tooby, E. Gould and W. Daniels; choristers by Messrs. T. H. Warburton, C. H. Nall, F. Leather, H. P. Nixon (organist), H. Burrows (ex-choirmaster) and seven boys. Floral tributes were sent by :—Wife and children, Father and family, Brother Sam and wife and children, Colleagues of the Post Office, Recto, Warden. Choir and Bellringers of War- burton Parish Church; Mrs. Grange and Miss Frith, Aunt Hannah and Cousins at Warbur- ton,. Mr. and •Mrs. J. Moreton, Mr. and Mrs. W. Travis and friends at "Farmer's," Mr. and Mrs. R. Walker, Neighbours, Green Dragon Bowling Club, Sam and Fanny, Uncle Abel and Aunt Mary and family,. Winifred Car- rington, F. Thornicroft, Mr. and Mrs. W. H. Jones, Mr. and Mrs. G. Wareham; Jack, Sid. and Wilf.; Jack and Ciss Wareham, Mrs. L. T. Barnes and Girls at Altrincham, Mr. and Mrs. Ronan, Saracen's Head; Corporal and Mrs. Burrows, and Mrs. Hulme.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>It DISTRICT /inn (.1</head>
        <p>It DISTRICT /inn (.1</p>
        <p>l_d Y MM</p>
        <p>LOCAL FACTS AND FANCIES.</p>
        <p>By CRITIC.</p>
        <p>Fun ran fast and furious at Oughtrington House last Saturday. Everything was up to perfection, and skilfully and ably managed.</p>
        <p>The clerk of the weather rose to the occasion, and the brilliant sunshine added to the splendour of the proceedings.</p>
        <p>Many. were the tales of romance told in the palmist's tent, and many old maids were again given hope.</p>
        <p>Every tale told meant an extra shilling to- wards a'shirt for "Tommy" or "Jack."</p>
        <p>The various concerts proved a great attrac- tion, and never failed to draw an enthusiastic audience.</p>
        <p>I afterwards learned that Mr. Brogdale, the mystery and wonder man, was also an amateur and, what is more, a local man.</p>
        <p>Tie took up conjuring for a hobby some time ago, and so successful did he turn out, that he is now capable of giving a thrilling and inter- esting entertainment.</p>
        <p>Who took away a wrong umbrella and kft a better one in mistake?</p>
        <p>Councillor Parker would be pleased if the guilty one will kindly forget.</p>
        <p>I was pleased to see Mr.llard. our worthy clerk to the Council, getting about and looking himself once again.</p>
        <p>The 113th anniversary of the Wesleyan Sun- day school was held last week-end.</p>
        <p>The vocal evening held in the chapel last Monday night created considerable interest in the villaga.</p>
        <p>• Mr. John Brazendale had again been success- ful in engaging some of the finest artistes in Manchester, including the old favourite, Miss Ada McHugh.</p>
        <p>Mr. Stanley Jones, a young but progressing organist, delighted the large audience with several recitals executed in a most efficient manner.</p>
        <p>The school children are now enjoying their annual summer holidays, which, I believe, stretches over a period of five weeks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>OUGHTRINGTON HOUSE.</head>
        <p>OUGHTRINGTON HOUSE.</p>
        <p>Garden Party Raises 110 For</p>
        <p>Soldiers' Comforts.</p>
        <p>Oughtrington House, a pretty mansion, situated next to the beautiful old church, was last Saturday the scene of a brilliant garden party, organised and conducted by a committee of Oughtrington 'people for the purpose of raising funds for comforts for our gallant soldiers and sailors. Thp house, with its spacious grounds, was kindly placed at the dis- posal of the committee. by Messrs. .Lever Bros., Ltd., who bought the 'Oughtrington estate a few years ago. On entering the grounds the casual visitor was confronted by attractions on every side. The appearance of ' Tommy ' from Brook- field, who was an invited guest, was a powerful reminder of the object of the party—the pro- vision of comforts for such men as these, who have gone oue and risked all to help to keep the old flag flying, and the old country from disgrace. The Attractions.</p>
        <p>During the day very successful concerts were rendered by the Oughtrington School Operetta Party. The artistes were little school children, and the skilful way in which they performed their various parts did great credit to their trainer, Miss Pennington. The miscellaneous concerts were conducted by the following artistes</p>
        <p>Mr. Brymer rendered in a rich bass voice "Lighterman Tom," &amp;c. Miss Hegg, a well- known ideal reciter, rendered with great effect "The Day," &amp;c.; and the Misses Peggy Moffat and Constance Hale gave a series of fancy, dances, ineftiding the tambourine dance, Welsh dance and sword dance._ ' Mr. O. G. Smith officiated at the piano. Mr. Beaver's concert party gave a _fine musical entertainment, and did not fail to win the applause of an appreci- ative audience. An interesting conjuring entertainment was also given by Mr. Brogdale, 6f Heatley, and some of the tricks he performed were certainly smart and original. During the afternoon and evening the Dun- ham Moodhouses Prize Band played for open- ail! dancing, under the leadprship of Mr. T. W. Warrington. This was Mowed by fi whist drive in the inner regions of the house. After the whist drive the Manchester Special Police came 'to the foreground, and Mr. Pray, an ardent member of that distinguished corps, who had been on duty in the grounds all day, con- ducted an auction sale, which realised .24.</p>
        <p>The various Attractions and assistants were as follows :</p>
        <p>Buffet and Refreshment Rms.—Mrs. Tudor- Evans, Mrs. Kirk, Mrs. Blanes, Mrs. Travis, Mrs. G. Smith, Mrs.. Crawley, Mrs. Yarwood, Mrs. Westbrook, Mrs. Rowlinson, Mrs. Wyatt, Mrs. Blease, Mrs. Howard, Mrs. Priestner, Mrs. Mullineaux, Mrs. Shaw, Miss Lamb, Mrs. G. Cook, Mrs. C. Hayes, Mrs. Fray, Mrs. Bent, Mrs. Kay, Mrs. 'Ball, Mrs. Clare, jun.; Misses Telfer, Miss Carr, Misses V. and M. Smith, Miss W. Burgess and Miss E. Renshaw. Aunt Sally Stall.—Mr. and Mrs. Joe Webb, and Mr. Bryden. Cocoanut StalL—Mr. Travis.</p>
        <p>Shooting Range.—Messrs. F. Rowlinson, W. R. Priestner, E. Crawley and R. A Yarwood. Fish Pond.—Mr. L. Critchley. Bombardo Table.—Mr. L. Barnes. Electric Battery.—Mr. J. Mullineaux. Clock Golf.—Mr. J. T. Clarke, who gave a prize for best Score. Palmistry.—Madame Zingari, who did a roaring trade amongst the ladies. Spider's Web.—Misses Priestner and Hegg. Ice Cream Stall.—Mrs. lifutton. 'Concerts.—Mr. Lamb and Mrs. Wyatt. Competition Table.—Miss Wild. Ice and Walnut.—Mrs. Kirk. Photographs (finished while you wait).—Mr. Ridge, Altrincham. Gate Keepers.—Messrs. C. Hayes, J. Mutton, G. Fenton, Lord, S. Broadey, T. Thomason, H. Jones and Jackson. Much help was also ren- dered by Messrs. Gavin Cooke, Brogdale, F. Ingham, Kirk, Mutton and IL tones. Fruit and Flowers.—Mrs. T. J. Ridgway, Mrs. Clark, Mrs. Gaskell, and the Misses Gould, Hankey and M. Davies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Prize-winners.</head>
        <p>Prize-winners.</p>
        <p>The various prize-winners were as follows:— Guessing Competitions.—Mr. Kirkham and Miss Pittman, both of Warrington; child's cot, Miss Southern, Lymm:, cake, Mrs. Peaks, Dun- ham; tea cloth, Mrs. Tudor Evans, Oughtring- ton; shawl, Mrs. Bell, Heatley; shooting (highest possible score 50) : ladies, Mrs. Hawkins, Lynam, 34; gentlemen, Private Bland, Brook- field Red Cross Hospital, 44; boys, D. Bryden,\ Oughtrington, 36. Whist Drive.—Ladies: Mrs. J. Eccleston and Miss Storrie. Gentlemen: Messrs. C. Forrest, F. A. Latham and B. Fox.</p>
        <p>The entire proceeds, which amount tct over £llO, are to be devoted to replenishing the funds of the Higher-lane Mission Room Working Party, which has met every week since the war began to make garments for our soldiers and sailors.</p>
        <p>The officials, who are to be congratulated upon the success of their undertaking, were :—Chair- man, Rev. G. L. Tudor-Evans; hon.. treasurer, Mrs. T. S. Ridgway; hon. secretary, Mr. L. Barnes; cashier, Mr. G. Telfer. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>MILITARY TRIBUNAL.</head>
        <p>MILITARY TRIBUNAL.</p>
        <p>Volunteer Training Corps And Exempted Men.</p>
        <p>Chairman, M. T. J. Ridgway; Military Representatives, Captain Hawkins, R.E., and Messrs. A. de C. F'armiter and, Newton Worth- ington.</p>
        <p>Wherever circumstances permit, the Lymm Tribunal are now granting exemptions subject to applicants joining Volunteer Training Corps. Coven Appeal Dismissed.</p>
        <p>An appeal' lodged by the Urban District Council for one of their carters on the grounds that he was indispensable, was dismissed. Volunteer Training Corps For Coal Merchant.</p>
        <p>A coal merchant who applied for absolute exemption, stated that his business was solely worked by himself. He had advertised it for sale, but had been unsuccessful. As a going concern it was worth .22.50. Hi 3 had done a lot of carting during the last four mon,ths for a firm who were engaged making ammunition boxes.—Are you a member of a Volunteer Training Corps?—No. I have not been a mem- ber for the last six months.—Are you willing to join?—Yes, I am quite willing to put in what time I can.—Exemption until November Ist, subject to joining a Volunteer Training Corps.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>Publican For Farm Work.</head>
        <p>Publican For Farm Work.</p>
        <p>A publican (35), appealed for absolute exemp- tion on the grounds that he had invested £7OO in business, and if he was called to the Colours his business would go smash, as his wife could not possibly manage it. Ile was quite willing to do work of national importance during the day time, and had offered his services to various farmers in the district. Mr. Alfred Keogh (solicitor), of Altrincham, represented appli- cant, and stated that applicant had been in this business since July, 1915. He had pre- viously kept a boarding-house at Blackpool, but had come over here to better himself. If applicant was allowed to do farm work in the day time he could look after his business at night, when the bulk of his trade was done.—ls your wife healthy?—Fairly.—Could not she manage the business?—lmpossible. She could not stillage barrels, &amp;c. —How many hours a day could you work on a farm?—About seven or eight, or an average day's work.—Are you willing to join a Volunteer Training Corps?—Yes! I am quite willing to do anything.—Exemption till Novem- ber Ist, subject to applicant undertaking to do farm work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>Far rer Receives Total Exemption.</head>
        <p>Far rer Receives Total Exemption.</p>
        <p>A farmer who occupies a farm-of 65 acres was granted conditional exemption, subject to his joining a Volunteer Training Corps.</p>
        <p>Small Farmers' Liabilities.</p>
        <p>Two small farmers, aged 3G and 27 respec- tively, stated that if they were called to the Colours they would suffer serious hardship They had invested all their life savings in business, and if they had to go it would all be lost. They occupied a small farm of 15 acres and kept seven milking cows, six pigs. and 40 head of poultry. They had broken two acres of land up with the spade, and were using the land for market gardening purposes.—What do you grow on the. land?—Cabbages, potatoes, onions; &amp;c.—Do you employ any men?—No. We do it between us.—Are you dependant on your earnings for a living?—Yes, Sir, wholly.—One was granted temporary exemption until Sep- tember Ist, and the other conditional exemption till November Ist, subject to his joining a Volunteer Training Corps., Aged Farmer's Appeal For Men..l</p>
        <p>• A grey haired farmer appeared to ask for exemption for his teamsman and cowman. He stated that he himself was 79 years of age next birthday, and he occupied a farm comprising 120 acres, '3O cows, five worling horses and three colts. He had one son living at home, and he was over military age.—Conditional exemption till November Ist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>DEATH OF MIL BEN DAVIES.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF MIL BEN DAVIES.</p>
        <p>Well-known Post Office Employe Passes Away.</p>
        <p>We regret to announce the death of Mr. Ben. Davies, which took place at. the Man- chester Infirmary last week-end. The late Mr. Davies was widely known in Lymra and dis- trict, for, though only forty years of age, he had served Heatley and Warburton with their postal correspondence twice daily for eighteen or twenty years. His cheery disposition won the hearts of all with whom he came into contact. The news of his death came as a great shock, for, prior to this last illness, he had enjoyed robust health. His life was one of great activity, and his hours of recreation were given up to three objects—walking, bell- ringing, and singing. In all three spheres he had gained the utmost success, .and he was the proud possessor of several challenge cups and certificates 'for walking. He was a prominent bellringer ,and chorister at the Warburton • Parish Church. He took a deep interest in the doings of the Post Office, and for several years was a member of the Postmen's Federation, a society run in the inteiests of all Post Office employes.. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>Funeral.</head>
        <p>The Funeral.</p>
        <p>The funerfl took place at the Oughtrington Parish Church On Tuesday afternoon and was witnessed by a large crowd of friends from Heatley, Warburton and Lymm. The Rev. Geoffrey Egerton-Warburton (rector of War- burton) and the Rev. Tudor Evans (rector- of Oughtrington) officiated. The body was con- veyed from Orchard-road, Sandy-lane, to the Oughtrington Church by the deceased's col- leagues from the, Post Office and his fellow- bellringers and the senior choristers from the Warburton Church, and was followed by his relatives and immediate friends. After the burial service had been held , the members of file Warburton Parish Church Choir sang "Now the labourer's task 'is o'er."</p>
        <p>The principal mourners were :—Mr. Sam Davies, Heatley (father); Mrs. Ben Davies (wife), Messrs. Sam, Alex. and, I. W. Davies and Private G. W. Davies (brothers); Mrs. S. Rutter, Heatley, and Mrs. J. T. Morgan, Heat-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>CHESIIIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</head>
        <p>CHESIIIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</p>
        <p>Lymm Platoon.</p>
        <p>Oiders for week ending July 22nd, 1916:</p>
        <p>Tuesday.—Shooting Practice at 7.30 p.m.; Platoon Drill at 8 p.m.; Enrolments, 8 to 9 p.m. Thursday.—Ditto ditto. ditto. Saturday.—Demonstration of trenches at Heaton Park. Pall-in at Oxford-road Station, Manchester, at 3.15 p.m. Train leaves Lymm at 2.41. Uniform; no arms. G. R. WILLIAMSON. Platoon Commander.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>LANCE-CORPL. JOSEPH ANTROBUS.</head>
        <p>LANCE-CORPL. JOSEPH ANTROBUS.</p>
        <p>Missing Man Reports Himself A Wounded Prisoner.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Antrobus, Yeald-brow, Statham, received a letter on Saturday from their son, Joseph, who was reported missing some weeks ago, along with Private Fred Moss, after a terrible bombardment, in which several Lymm men lost their lives. The gravest fears were entertained as to the fate of these two men. Happily, however, both men have communi- cated with their parents, and it is found that both are wounded and prisoners-of-war in Germany.</p>
        <p>Lance:Corporal J. .Antrobus is severely</p>
        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL JOSEPH ANTROBUS. Wounded Prisoner-of-War.</p>
        <p>wounded in. the right arm and right leg, which may have to be amputated. It is hoped, how- ever, that through this wound he will be in- cluded in the next batch of exchanged prisoners.</p>
        <p>Referring to the loss of his leg, he says : "But never mind, I have still got one left, and before long .I hope to be hobbling about again. I should very much like a parcel." Lance-Corporal Joseph Antrobus is but 20 years of age, and enlisted, in September, 1914. He was drafted out to France in September, 1915. Prior to the war he was a gold-beater in the employ of Messrs. William Wright and Sons, gold-beaters and refiners, of Newfields. He worshipped at the, Congregational Chapel, and was also a prominent member of the Lymm Lads' Club. He was educated at the I.4ymm National Schools.</p>
        <p>Honour For Lymm Gardener.—Mr. George Willan, of Redcot, the well-known landscape and fancy gardener, has just succeeded in win- ning the Horticultural Society's preliminary examination for the national diploma for horti- culture. The examination took place at London three weeks ago. Mr. Willful is the only suc- cessful candidate in this district; the next nearest known is a park gardener at Bradford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week ending 22nd July, 1916 Monday.—Musizetry at Range, 7.30 p.m.; range officer, Sect.-Com. Trantom.</p>
        <p>Tuesday.—Company Drill; fall-in at Head- quarters, 7.45 p.m. Friday.—Musketry at Range, 7.30 p.m.; range officer,Sect.-Com. Fox.</p>
        <p>Satniday.—Fall-in at Headquarters (uniform) at 2.45 p.m. for Company Drill, in conjunction with Stockton Heath and Warrington Detach- ments, in Latchford, as before. Orderlies.—Sect.-Com. Trantom and Private J. F. Fairhurst.</p>
        <p>F. A. FROST, 0.0.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE J. McINTYRE. Wounded.</p>
        <p>July in France. On Monday news came that he had been wounded and was lying in a London hospital, and fuller details followed. From these it appears that he was wounded in the lower part of the body and in the legs, while the top joint of the second finger on, his left hand was shot away. Such a number of serious wounds naturally caused much anxiety to his parents, and Mr. and Mrs. Mclntyre I..ft for London on Wednesday to visit their son in the Southwark Military Hospital, S.E., where he had neen re moved. It is understood he is going on favour- ably, Private Mclntyre was born in 1897 and enlisted in August, 1914, so that at present he is only nineteen years of age. During his train- ing he won laurels as a crack shot, making the top score in the whole battalion. He after- wards joined the Machine Gun Section, in which he was operating when wounded. This mar- vellously plucky youth was educated at St. Thomas' C.E. Schools, Stockton Heath, and the Grammar School, Warrington, and at the time of joining the Army was serving with Messrs. Wright and Hamly-n, architects. As a member of the cricket team, he won the bat in 1913 for the highest batting average. Mr. Mclntyre has another soldier-son in Lance-Corporal Frank. Mclntyre, of the 10th Liverpool Scottish. He is now in training in Shropshire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>PRIVATE JOHN PERSON.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE JOHN PERSON.</p>
        <p>Wounded A Second Time; Going On "All Right."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Pearson, 11, Derby-road, Stockton Heath, has received news from her son, Private John Pearson, of the Border Regiment, that he has been wounded in the great advance. He received a shrapnel bullet in his right shoulder, and, explaining the circumstances under which he got the wound, wrote: "I got my wound in the big advance in the second line trench. We took the first and then I felt something hit me in the back The bullet went in at</p>
        <p>the top of my shoulder and down my shoulder- blade." He was brought to a Manchoster hqs- pital, where the bullet was extracted, and Mrs. Pearson has been later informed that he is going on "all right." This is the second time Private Pearson (who</p>
        <p>is twenty-eight) has been wounded: On the first occasion, in May, he was struck on the head with a piece of shrapnel, but the wound providentially did not prove serious. For the latter he was treated at the base hospital in France and had only returned to the trenches a week prior to getting the second knock. Mrs. Pearson, who is a widow, has another son , working on munitions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week commencing 17th July, 1916, issued by Mr. Frank Warburton, O.C. : Monday.—Drill at Headquarters, 7.45.</p>
        <p>Tuesday.—Appleton Rifle Range: No. .1 Sec- tion„Class Firing; Nos. 1,2, 3 and 4 Sections, Squad practice "Volley Firing." The follow- ing members of the Recruit/Class are requested to attend at 6.30 for musketry instruction : Messrs. Taylor, Hazlehurst, Warburton, Chile, Fleming and Brayshaw. Thursday.—Drill at Headquarters, 7.45.</p>
        <p>To-day (Saturday).—Battalion Parade, Alder- ley Edge. Fall-in Victoria Square, 2.30 sharp; marching order; one ration to be carried.</p>
        <p>Missionary Work in Eastern Lands.—An in- teresting address was giv. n in the Parish Room on Monday evening, at the monthly meeting of the Missionary Association, by Miss Hutchings, of Frodsham, in which she enlarged upon the activities of women in the mission field. She gave an account of the position of the women in India, China and Persia, dwelling mort. particularly upon their status under Moham- medan rule. The great rogress already made could be traced to the unselfish efforts of early women micsionaries, and Miss Humphries laid special emphasis on the point that at the con- clusion of the war there would be an increased demand for workers, doctors, nurses, and others. It was hoped the present revival in women's work and influence all over the world would be a Christian movement with high ideals. A vote of thanks was accorded to Miss Hutchings for her address, and conveyed in 'complimentary terms by the Rev. J. Collins, vicar.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>GREAT SANKEY.</head>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY.</p>
        <p>VICAR'S SON KILLED.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant J. H. R. Jones Falls In Great Advance.</p>
        <p>Very deep and genuine sympathy is felt by all his parishioners and a large circle of other friends for the respected Vicar Of Sankey ,(the Rev. J. R Jones) in his great 'bereavement—the loss of his only surviving son, Lieutenant John Harold Ryle Jones. The sad tidings that Lieutenant Jones had been killed reached the Vicarage first of all—as, indeed, it happens in many similar cases—through a letter received by another person from the scene of conflict. Anxious inquiry of the War Office confirmed the melancholy news, and on Wednesday morning the following telegram was received i--" Rev. Jones, MA., Sankey Vicarage, Warrington.— Deeply regret to inform you that report just received states Second-Lieut. J. H. R. Jones, South Lancashire Regiment, was killed in action July 4th. The Army Council express their sym- pathy.—Secretary, War Office." _ Lieutenant Jones has thus fallen gallantly in</p>
        <p>Ns hat will doubtless go down in history as "the great advance." This young officer—he was only 27—has had a brilliant and in many re- spects an interesting career, full of extraordin- ary promise. Born in Widnes, he was educated at the Warrington Grammar School and subse- quently at the. Liverpool College, passing from thence into Oxford University (Jesus College). He possessed high scholastic attainments and carried off many distinctions. He was a pro- ficient linguist and was especially well equipped in French and German, having lived for some time on the Continent. It was thought at first that he would have entered the Church, but his mind was eager for more vigorous and strenuous activities, and, needless to say, his inclinations were respected. Great Traveller and Sportsman.</p>
        <p>He went out to Western Canada, after travel- ling in Europe, and was occupied in surveying on the Canadian Pacific Railroad—being for some time at Calgary. While in Canada he joined the 101st Edmonton Fusiliers, and on the outbreak of war, so eager was he to fight for the Mother Country, that he vralked„a dis- tance of 70 miles—.from Lae-labiehe to Atha- baska—in Order to enlist. He came over with the Canadian contingent, and when it was known that while at Oxford he had been a sergeant in the University Officers' Training Corps, he was given a commission as Second. Lieutenant in a battalion of the South Lanca- shire Regiment. Lieutenant Jones was a Pro- minent athlete and won_mapy.,tropliies_vcw at Oxford University. His bereaved father showed the writer, with loving and pardonable pride, a row of beautiful silver mpg won' by his</p>
        <p>gallant son on many a hotly-contested field. ,1 few of the inscriptions may be interesting : Jesus College Sports, 1908.—Half-mile. Lincoln College Sports, 1908, Stranger? Race.—One Mile Handicap (2nd). Jesus College Sports, 1908.—High .Jump. Exeter College Sports, 1908, Strangers' Race. —One Mile Handicap (2nd). Jesus College Sports, 1909.-300 Yards. Jesus College Sports, 1909.—Quhrter-mile. He carried off many other prizes for different events. He rowed and played football for hi° College and was one of the successful team which secured pride of place in rifle shooting competitions. In his younger days, he played "Rugger" for Stockton Heath.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Our Fighting Men.—The arrivals of fighting men include John Pusill, K.R.R.; HarrY Goulden, South Lanes.; and Corporal Herbert Molyneux, Rifle Brigade. The last named bias been under treatment during the last five montlo for trench feet, incurred whilst doing his bit at the Front.</p>
        <p>_ The departures are :—Marshall Whitfield, Albert Bates and Joseph Parker. Sam Jones and Fred Kimberley have been made corporals.</p>
        <p>Walking Day.—Last Saturday saw the Penketh Wesleyan. Sunday School children celebrating their Walking Day in a manner befitting the present conditions Dispensing with the usual , procession through and around the village, they gathered in. the schoolroom at four o'clock for their tea. Immediately that was finished they transferred themselves to the recreation ground, where various contests Suitable to their fig° were carried out, games being found for those whose inclinations were of a less strnuous order. Returning to the schoolroom at eig_ht o'clock, the winners were presented 1;ly Mrs. vv• - Moores with those prizes which elastic musees and a determination to succeed had won. for them. Members of the Men's Bible Class de- voted their time and attention to the children's general enjoyment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>BOWLING MAIN.</head>
        <p>BOWLING MAIN.</p>
        <p>Last Saturday Penketh Tannery played their return main at Heath-road, succumbing to the "Heathens" by 83 points, although on the home and home games the tanners are leaders by five points. The scores were : Penketh Tannery: John Bowyer, 21; Js'ne Bowyer, 18; J. Higham, 21; A. Bowyer, 4; ,14.° Goulden, 11; J. Barlow, 4; T. Gregson, 21; Bowyer, 17; J. Rigby, 21; W. Shave, 15; Scott, 8; J. Atkinson, 16; H. Bowyer, 14; Houghton, 21; T. Jackson, 4; 11. Glover, 1; total, 217.</p>
        <p>Heath-road: T. Darlington, 12; H. Dobbins, 21 ; 'S. Tunstall, 17; P. Johnson, 21; W. Light- foot, 21; R. Graham, 21; F. Johnson, 9; T. Jack- son, 21; C. Monks, 16; E. Glover, 21; S. Tunstall, 21; W. Smith, 21; T. Jackson, 21; L. 'Monks, P; W. Lightfoot, 21; A. James, 21; total, 300. It was necessary to duplicate in several 'lll- - to make the requisite number of player'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>Route March.—ln view of the forthcoraiag„ route march and camp in the early days August, a tramp to,Lymra was arranged by Of_ 2nd Pa,dgate Scouts on Saturday afternoon. P' charge of their Scoutmaster (Mr. W. E.. Plias- ton) and Assistant Scoutmaster (Mn. Yir. 11. Bate) the troop left headquarters at 2.30, Prc: ceeding by way of Green-lane, Martin.scrin Woolston and across the ferry to Thelwall anr Lymm. A halt was made at Messrs. Egerton-5 C.T.C. farm, where tea was provided and genies' &amp;c., were played in the field. Recreation over' the troop marched through the village, alte,l: which they were dismissed and allowed to lie" the various places of interest. The returili journey was commenced at seven o'clock, an° home was reached at nine o'clock, after a ver/ enjoyable afternoon's outing.</p>
        <p>Boy Scout's Accident.—On Monday, MasteAr Eric Walton, one of the members of the 211: Padgate Scouts, was returning to school, whem he ran behind a passing lorry and hung on ter the back of it. When passing the Folic: Station he got his foot fast between the sPriiit and the wheel, the result being that the was unable to go round. A number of 1"i...- school children saw the accident, drew the t- tention of the driver, who pulled up imrnte ew- ately. Assistance was obtained, but it was minutes before the boy could be extricated. 111: was taken into the Police Station, where firs„ aid was,rendered by P.C. Edelsten. Afterwr. aZ' he was removed home and attended by Beenett. It was found that no bones vq,e.'ll broken, but there were severe abrasions, %clue will take time to heal. , Christ Church Anniversary.—The day Sunday school sermons in connection with Church were preached on Sunday, the preacvL in the morning, being Archdeacon Howson, service being conducted by the Rey. 14 girso Speaking from Exodus xiv., 15, the ArchdeaVh referred to the time of testing through "glue we are passing. as The Rev. G. Vale Owen, vicar of Orford, w,,0 the preacher at the evening service, and "ne. spoke on 1 Samuel, xiv., 3. Referring to ed,llc 0, tion, he said that the main idea is to egelyieo lad to get on in the world, to get position, and power. He suggested as remedies for eclll'..; lion: Rip up the time table; no examination;o put a good man in the school and trust billion. bring up the children to be good men or weid. In the afternoon a children's service was of conducted by the Rev- J. Hirst. Gifts an flowers, eggs, cigarettes and other comforts licio brought by the children. for the wel2! to soldiers. These were taken in the eveliA,7ol6 the Barracks and Whitecross Military ilelos in Mr. Gorton's motor, by Miss Gorton, Harter and Mrs. J. Clare. cisi At the morning and evening services sPeoro. music was rendered by the choir. In the ,I,llrdl' ing the anthem "The Lord is My ShePile._ je and Winchester's Te Deum and Benedictles F were given; and in the evening ge,il. solo hymn anthem "0 Worship the King " loist and chorus were very well rendered, the being Miss MiSs E. Critchley. Mr. F. Blacklireu4tit sided at the organ. The offertories througo the day amounted to upwards of XS.</p>
      </div>
