      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>PICTURISED ROMANTIC NOVEL BY ROY L.McCARDELL. ADAPTED FROM THE SENSATIONAL- FLYING A- SERIAL FILM BY ALEC J. 8RA.1.1).</head>
        <p>PICTURISED ROMANTIC NOVEL BY ROY L.McCARDELL. ADAPTED FROM THE SENSATIONAL- FLYING A- SERIAL FILM BY ALEC J. 8RA.1.1).</p>
        <p>[The characters In this story are purely imaginary living</p>
        <p>SYNOPSIS OF PRECEDING CHAPTERS.</p>
        <p>, Colonel Arthur Stanleigh and Judge Lamar Stan- sigh, cousins, of Virginia, are bitter enemies over 'no Possession of The Diamond from the Sky, a massive jewel heirloom found centuries ago in a fallen meteor. Colonel Stanleigh, when a daughter Is born, buys the male.heir of a gipsy king, that he nMY keep the jewel. He plans to rear Esther, the girl, in secrecy. Esther and The Diamond from the Sky are stolen by Hagar, the gipsy mother. Years later Hagar returns Esther and the diamond, but of the old group only Dr. Lee is living. Blair Stanleigh kills Dr. Lee and steals the jewel. Then Blair Stanleigh and Arthur Stanleigh, the false heir, fight a duel. Arthur, thinking he has killed his cousin, flees in a racing motor car. In his Mad flight the machine goes over a river bank, and that night the moon shines on a body floating down the stream. Arthur is found by Esther and Hagar and nursed back to health. Hagar tells him his real identity. Arthur swears to make himself Worthy of-Esther and leaves, taking the diamond, and meets Blair Stanleigh. Blair takes him to a grand hall, where they meet Vivian Marston, a New York belle, wearing the diamond. Hagar is there, too, telling fortunes. Vivian has just seated herself, when a brawny forearm clutches The 33iantond from the Sky. A greater confusion than ever that caused by the loss of the diamond arose When the Sheriff of Fairfax arrived at the ball-and neraanded the arrest of Arthur Stanleigh. He "aeaPed, partly through the efforts of Hagar, and lat.er found work and refuge as a farm hand. Luke Lovell dropped the diamond into a mail-bag. The a„em clung by its clasp to a rent in the bag, and t" bag was afterwards found by Quabba, the hunchback organ-grinder. He does not retain it lOng. Clarence, his monkey, drops it in an owl's nest. Arthur, working on a farm as "John 130Nvell," masks himself and goes to the Fairfax tournament, where he wins highest honour. He is betraYed by Blair Stanleigh, and, amid, great con- fusion, struggles madly to escape.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER VII</p>
        <p>TEE PDX AND THE PIG</p>
        <p>Pilled with bitter resentment the young Vir- ginians had fought to bar Arthur's escape. But the rider had come to the tournament conscious of The risk he ran, and had taken the only chance of escape that remained. Not for show had he trained himself to become a skilled horseman. Never had tsich a feat of horsemanship been seen in Fairfax— same of daring riders. , Twenty miles away the Monticello hunt was rid- to hounds. Arthur's dramatic escape had given nun considerable lead over his pursuers, and fifteen Miles from the tournament ground, with the sheriff and several of the competitors toiling along in his track, the fugitive quieted Starlight to a less strenuous pace. Noting that the way the huntsmen 'were taking was in the direction of Farmer Smith's laro. Arthur turned that way. aust ahead of him, in a hollow at the bottom of be field by a sunken fence, Arthur beheld the last ef the huntsmen make a leap with his horse, only 1° come a nasty cropper, and to lie in a huddled strangely still. The hunter's horse stopped L'Tith an abrupt jerk as the fallen reins caught and 4,1,1 him tightly by one of the gnarled branches °I an old tree. 41-rthur rode over and dismounted. The hunter Zaa dead, his face bruised and disfigured where it struck the log, and his neck was broken. ...1:11en a desperate idea seized Arthur. This man ,sisas dead ,and beyond all aid.. He hurriedly anged his coat, with its white scarf, and his ,711/ned hat for the red hunting-jacket and velvet Zap of the dead fox-hunter. Then, engaging the idle- of Starlight to the gnarled limb, from which te had released the dead hunter's horse, he changed hie mount and cantered away. When Sheriff Swain and his posse of tournament iders reached the scene, they saw a fox-hunter in nis red coat riding far away, but at their feet lay, B, s it seemed to them, the body of Arthur Stanleigh, killed by a fall from the horse he had ridden so gallantly. Cropping the grass near the log against which seemingly his rider had fallen, the bridle rein Caught in a knotted branch of the fatal tree, -was the horse that they all recognised as the very steed that had made the leap from the grandstand, a leap Ong to be remembered. saving thus gained a welcome respite, Arthur 'sae to the farm and told all his story to Farmer Strath. The kind-hearted man understood, and that night the young roan left the hmal with " Good-bye and good luck!" ringing in his ears as harbinger of glad tidings. The body of the poor, crushed hunter was con- l'eYed to Stanleigh Hall. Luke Lovell heard the „Ilesfs and came with it to Hagar. "They are bring- ing Arthur Stanleigh here!" Tearfully Esther and Hagar awaited the arrival ef the body of the man who, in spite of his weak- !leases. they loved. His frailties might be many, but his manliness and daring at the tournament 'wined out the memory of the past. With a glad cry Esther recognised that it was )10, Arthur, but Hagar quickly silenced her. " A RiDey's trick," she murmured. "Let them find out the ruse in .all good time. It will give him a better 9iance to get far away." Within a few hours the ttuth came out. One of the huntsmen was missing, and his body found at Stanleigh Hall. Whether her son acted as he should have done not, Hagar did not intend that the stigma of 111.1ntder should be over his head. She firmly be- -I"ed that Blair Stanleigh knew more than he Z!`red to reveal, and Tom Blake continued to pursue 18 inquiries. One day he came to the hall with a ?ertfolio of curious photographs. They were the tell-tale reproductions of Blair Stanleigh's finger- brinte. Some were made by Blake from impres- !isns he found on the rifled cashbox and the dead ii,Petor's throat, and one other was the reproduc- I:sn of the mark of an inkstained thumb, stained a leaky fountain-pen, made on the bad cheque Passed on Abe Bloom, gambling-den keeper of ?le/anion&amp; The cheque came back to the vindic- "-e. angry gambler marked "No funds "; and A_en Abe, Bloom called upon the Blake Detective eneY to aid him in recovering the amount of the 'clue the wily Tom Blake became in possession of &gt;eettecercermee 040 _</p>
        <p>',Of)</p>
        <p>, and have no connection with or relation to any person.]</p>
        <p>the convincing identification he had been seeking— the duplicate thumb-print of the murderer of Dr. Lee. The thumb-print of Blair Stanleigh! The astute if not overscrupulous detective was working only for his „client, Hagar Harding, who paid him well. There was no reward offered for the apprehension of Blair. Stanleigh or any other man save the innocent Arthur Stanleigh. Detec- tive Blake knew that Hagar would use the evi- dence he brought her in her own way. It con- cerned him no further, for the prejent at least, and he took his fee and returned to Richmond on the same train that bore Mrs. Burton Randolph "in a state of nervous depression," and the ever vivacious Vivian Marston. He sat behind them and smiled grimly as he heard them utter the name of Blair Stanle,igh, "such a dear boy!"</p>
        <p>After the departure of the detective Hagar ordered her carriage. "1 am going to pay a call. I shall not be long," she said to Esther. Hagar was going to call upon Mrs. Lamar Stanleigh and confront that haughty woman with irrefutable evi- dence of her son's guilt. The judge's widow and Blair had returned from witnessing the departtire of Mrs. Randolph and Vivian. The yound man on the way back resolved to follow the gay New Yorker. and at the house went straight to the hiding-place to rifle his mother's cash-box. He allowed nothing to stand in his way once his mind was made up. Sitting on the piazza awaiting Blair's return Mrs. Stanleigh was musing on the past. "What is the use?" she kept saying to herself. "What is the use?" What use the ambition, the cold. caloulat- ing ambition that had darkened her life and had caused the tragic death of her husband eighteen long years ago?</p>
        <p>What was the use to scheme and plot and hope and hate for a bauble that had disappeared—The Diamond from the Sky—and an earldom further away than ever. The diamond and the earldom were not for her. They were never to be the pos- session of her son either, it would seem. Dead men lay between, and a living man, a wanderer and a fugitive; and the earl, old and feeble, a helpless invalid for yenta, still lingered on. Arthur Stan- leigh, proscribed as a murderer, though he was, bore a charmed life that stood between her son and the earldom and the diamond.</p>
        <p>Amid her musings the judge's widow saw com- ing towards her Hagar Harding, the new mistress of Stanleigh Hall. What could the woman want? At the sight of Hagar all the old hopes and all the old hatreds leaped again within her breast. She rose as if to enter her.house and ignore the woman, but Hagar stayed her with a gesture. "Do not go. Mrs., Stanleigh," said she in even tones. "I have with me proofs that your son, and not Arthur Stanleigh, is guilty of the murder of Dr. Henry Lee." ..</p>
        <p>"Hush!" whispered the judge's widow tensely. " Come inside!" and she led her strange guest within the portals of her borne and up to the living room, behind a wall of which Blair crouched in fear of discovery,</p>
        <p>" Now tell me what you mean by saying that my son killed Dr. Lee. If you make such a statement you 'should have proof." " I have, and here it is," holding out the photo- graphs. " Those are the thumb-prints of Blair Stanleigh, your son!" "I do not believe it. My son is no murderer."</p>
        <p>"This evidence is too strong. See this dis- honoured cheque, drawn by your son in favour of a gambling-house keeper. That smudge is his thumb-print." "What price do you ask for silence? I cannot think you would come here except to bargain." "My price," replied Hagar, "is an easy one. I ask that you and all your friends receive my daughter Esther and myself in Fairfax. I have only this to say: deem me whom you may, my daughter Esther is Of as high birth and blood as the proudest families of Fairfax!" "I will get my son. He is somewhere about the house," said Mrs. Stanleigh, as she left the room.</p>
        <p>_ In his hiding-place behind the chimney Blair Stanleigh had heard .every word. In his hands he clutched a mass of bank-notes.. Thrusting them in his pocket he touched the spring, and noiselessly pushed aside the swinging fireplace. Hagar stood by the table, her back to the fire- place, watching the door. On the table were the incriminating photographs of the thumb-prints of Blair Stanleigh. As she turned at the sound of Blair's advance, he struck her down with the heavy iron poker, and she fell to the floor as though lifeless, and he seized the photographs.</p>
        <p>Hagar moaned and stirred. Blair could hear his mother calling him in the hallway. He seized the handcuffs the sheriff had left with him, and clasped them on the unresisting wrists of the unconscious woman. Picking her up he thrust her in the hid- ing-place and hastily swung the wall back into position, leaving the manacled form of Hagar im- prisoned by the broken open chest in the darkened niche.</p>
        <p>At that instant, hearing his mother on the threshold, he turned and fled.</p>
        <p>Miles away a coloured boy was playing that he was a hunter after eagles. He had a wooden gun, this coloured boy who herded pigs, and he was now to play a part in this strange drama.</p>
        <p>Beneath a wayside tree a handful of feathers attracted his attention. To his keen eye there were evidences of a scramble, and the feathers were proof that somewhere up the tree was an owl's nest. This active youth had robbed many nests in his time, and with a conclusive "Owls up dar," he climbed the tree. From a fork in the tree an owl flew with a querulous screech. Reaching in his hand for an egg, his fingers fastened upon a chain and jewel. Once more the diamond had changed hands. That evening the urchin, whose lot it :vas to ter.d a farmer's numerous swine, was bianing; ove: a sty. dangling before an unappreciative pig The Diamond from the Sky. But the boy was fully as dense to the value of the priceless gem as was the porker. Walking slowly through the farmyard came Strap McGee, the tramps' leader, whose purloining of Arthur Stan- leigh's evening suit led to his arrest. A free man again, he was seeking unconsidered trifles. (To be continued.) MeeteefrOftbeiteCeeeCeeeeMi</p>
        <p>The Great Flying -A rial Film.</p>
      </div>
