      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 15, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Flag Days. I would not for a moment be understood a deprecating the Warrington flag day project, but I feel sure many readers will agree with me that it is wellnigh time that local com- mittees cudgelled their wits to devise some fresh method of money-raising for charitable purposes. Excellent as it was, the idea is by way of being played out, and all concerned are longing for a change. I spent the week-end in West Yorkshire industrial districts. On Satur- day I touched at Halifax, and found that there it was pansy day. Although I was not stopping there at all, of course I had to sport a con- spicuous pansy; it was the only effectual weapon of defence, for the flower girls were here, there and everywhere. I was bent on a car ride to Bradford and back—a 16-mile circu- lar tour over breezy altitudes—and I cannot say I was in the least surprised to discover that there it was Red Cross flag day, and that my pansy -so longer availed me at all. My car was boarded en route by an enterprising maiden armed with flags and the usual money box, and by sheer force of will she had the whole load of passengers decorated in double quick time. When we reached our journey's end, there were quite a lot of 'em tarred with the same brush awaiting us in eager anticipation, but of course they were doomed to disappointment; with looks of mingled amazement and disgust they melted away like the Cheshire cat in ."Alice," to materialise as soon as the next lot of pros- pective victims appeared in sight.</p>
        <p>Rough on the Germans. - Bradford has always had its big German colony, and, as I read .in last week's leader about punishing the enemy, I thought of at least one tradesman in th&amp;city in question who is .already doing his bit in that direction. In his shop window I saw, a large. card on which was 'printed in prominent characters the announcement—" We do not want the custom of Germans; -naturalised or -otherwise, and we trUst they will keep outside this establishment." Unmistakably plain even to bluntness—what one may term a straight tip, eh? Cremation,:</p>
        <p>Bradford has its municipal crematorium, and I made the rather long journey out of the city for the purpose of inspecting it. I was shown everything, and the entire process was minutely explained to me by an expert, and I came away more than ever convinced that this is by far the better way of disposing of the dead. I saw, too, what purported to be photographs of earth-buried bodies after three, six, nine, and twelve months'. interment. All I will say is that, if these fairly represented the condition of things in an ordinary cemetery, the whole place must be a seething mass of indescribable ‘'or- ruption. Altogether, I imagine that if my readers could see what I saw on Saturday, it would need no further argument to convert many of them to my way of thinking. \ _ Cranks.</p>
        <p>To be sure, there are strange faddists among the believers in cremation, just as there are among vegetarians, Esperantists, conscientious objectors and the like. When I read of one who directed that his cremated remains should be carried out to sea and there dispersed, I marvelled as at a singular mortal; but it ap- pears that those who would forthwith be for- gotten and leave no sort of record of their existence here below are not few. In the form of instructions to those in charge of the last obsequies is a column in which one is required to state how it is proposed to dispose of the ashes after cremation, and I was told that it is not uncommon to read in this the curt entry "Scatter to the. winds"—an order which I was assured is always literally obeyed. Others who are not quite so indifferent request to be "buried in the bushes," which means that the urn is interred in a plot reserved for the ashes of those who do not wish to incur further expense, and yet wish the remains to be preserved intact In mother earth. In "Thumbnail Essays," "MC." says that erati- fied vanity is the form of happiness which the world prefers before all others, and that there is something in it those of us who have been behind the scenes in public life know full well; still, it is clear that there are superior mortals who, are incapable of being moved by any such considerations, and who reveal their true feel- ings unmistakably when it comes to making known their final wishes.</p>
        <p>• No Lasting Fame. After all, the most sanguine and confident among us will hardly dare to entertain the hope that the next generation but one will be aware that they ever lived. Only a small minority have learned the secret of enduring fame. Nixon Waterman puts the whole thing in a nutshell in some lines which my memory has long cherished. Thus— "Oh, life? how very brief your span! How small the part I play! This wondrous thing they call a man Is but a piece of clay. How very soon I'll be forgot When I, like all, must die. .Ary own great-great-grandchild may not E'en know where I shall lie.</p>
        <p>And shall they thus in memory My life so lightly keep? Will not my own remember me When in the grave I sleep? The thought is sad, and yet, howe'er I dare not criticise. Since I don't really know just where My great-great-grandpa lies."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>"DARTMOOR SHEPHERD."</head>
        <p>"DARTMOOR SHEPHERD."</p>
        <p>Twelve Months' Imprisonment For The "Hopeless One."</p>
        <p>"Here Lloyd George's gentle shepherd stands!"</p>
        <p>The reference woyld have been appropriate at the Borough Police Court on Saturday, when David Davies, the aged , and notorious thief from Wales, stood in the dock on a charge of stealing -10 s. 10d. froni the poor box at the Sacred Heart Presbytery, and was sentenced to twelve months' imprisonment. Davies, who is well over seventy years old, pleaded guilty to the theft, and then added that on entering the church he had no inten- tion of committing the offence. The Chief Constable (Mr. Martin Nicholls): I don't suppose he had, but it is 'impossible for him to go straight. He only came out of gaol last month. He haS been in Warrington before and I offered to find him work and get him to go straight, but he preferred to return to Liverpool. In explaining the offence to which Davies pleaded guilty, the Chief Constable said that on the Friday night week the prisoner was seen in the church by Winifred Donahue, who is a young lady employed by Father Rockliffe. She saw Davies taking pennies out of his hat and slipping them into his pocket, and there- fore warned Father Rockliffe, who handed him over to Special . Constables Wainwright and Kay. In addition to the 10s. 10d. in his posses- sion he had over</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>Terrible Record.</head>
        <p>A Terrible Record.</p>
        <p>4.` Although not by sight," added the Chief Constable, "Davies is well known to the Bench by reputation. For many years past he ' has been Spoken of as 'The Dartmoor Shep- herd,' and you will recollect that some eminent Members of, Parliament came across him, and, believing that he had been too heavily sen- tenced, released him. But, your Worships, it seems absolutely impossible that_ the Prisoner can go straight and lead an honest life. At the present there is hanging over his head a Detention Order for ten years, which, for some reason or other, has never been enforced. On the sth of January of this year Davies was sent to gaol by the Liverpool magistrates for six months for a similar offence to that to which he has just pleaded guilty." The Chief Constable then detailed the long list of crime for which Davies has been convicted, the sentences including one of fifteen years, an- other of ten, and several of five years' penal servitude, particularly for thefts from churches and housebreaking. "Your case is a hopeless one," remarked the Chairman of the Bench (Mr. John Fairclough) in passing sentence. Davies merely sniffed and walked leisurely down the steps out of the limelight.</p>
      </div>
