      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>BRITISH CROWN GREEN COUNTY CHAMPIONSHIP.</head>
        <p>BRITISH CROWN GREEN COUNTY CHAMPIONSHIP.</p>
        <p>" Crosfleld " Challenge Cup.</p>
        <p>Matches in connection with the above cup were resumed on Saturday betwee4 Lancashire and Staffordshire, and Cheshire and Derbyshire. Both Lancashire and Cheshire returned aggregate wins. Cheshire by 105 points, and Lancashire by 76 points. For the last named club J. Smith, of Heatley, scored a good victory of 21-18 over R. Reynolds, of New Mills.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <p>REPORTS FROM THE FIELDS OF PLAY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCH.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCH.</p>
        <p>LIBERAL CLUB v. COMMERCIALS.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of the bowling match held last Saturday at the Liberal Club, when the visiting team, the Commercial Travellers, were beaten by 116 points, a pleasant function took place, at which Mr. Robert Atherton was presented with a valu- able umbrella by the committee in recognition of the good work done by him for the bowling green. Mr. Atherton (Mrs. Whitfield's brother, who has been spending his holidays in Warrington) put in a good deal of time in trimming the green and surrounding foliage, as well as brightening up the garden.</p>
        <p>Alderman D. Tinnion, who presided, supported by Mr. Harry Walsh, first of all spoke of the future of their friends and erstwhile opponents, the Commercial Travellers, and expressed the hope that they would accomplish great things in develop- ing trade with our Allies. He laid stress upon the necessity of studying the languages of the friendly nations, as such knowledge would prove of great advantage to them. He heartily welcomed the visitors to the club.</p>
        <p>Mr. Harry Walsh. in reply, thanked the members of the club for their invitation and hospitality. Although beaten on this occasion on the green they would have another try. A vote of thanks was then passed to Mr. and Mrs. Whitfield for the excellent manner in which they had carried out the catering. At this stage the presentation was made to Mr. Atherton by Alderman Tinnion, who xpressed the thanks of the club in his usual happy style. Mr. Atherton, in reply, said it was a great pleasure to him to do what he had done. The whole details of the match were in the hands of Mr. Harry Walsh and Mr. George Marshall, and they were also thanked for their services. Scores:—</p>
        <p>Liberal Club. Commercial Travellers. _ S. D. S. D.</p>
        <p>Alderman D. Tinnion (capt.) 11 H. Walsh J. Pollard . ... 11 15 A. Allen ... 4 0 R. Whitfield .. 11 F. Howard A. Lingard .... 10 15 G. Bowman J. Pollitt Wm. Boyle T. Harrison J. Stanley Joe Bennion Wm. Daintith 4 15 W. L. Walker 11 12 Councillor J. T Locker P. Walker Wm. Eyes G. Marshall .Tos. Wilkinson 11 J. Harvey ..... 9 T. Williams J. Evans H. Johnson A. Podmore T. Critchley T. Dutton ..... 11 C. Hayes T. Drew ...... 11 11 J. Gandy ..... M. Llewellyn .. 11 W. Dakin J. Parr 166 123</p>
        <p>235 168</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>BDRUIDS' ANNUAL *MAIN.</head>
        <p>BDRUIDS' ANNUAL *MAIN.</p>
        <p>The Order of Druids Friendly Society held their ninth annual bowling main at the Plough Inn, Fearnhead, on Saturday, when the silver cup. kindly given by Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., and other prizes, were bowled for. Miss Derbyshire had the green in splendid condition, and a pleasant afternoon's sport was thoroughly enjoyed. The winner of the cup and first prize was Bro. W. Thomasson. of the "Druids' Camp" Lodge, who beat Bro. J. Daintith of the same Lodge by 13-8. Other prize winners were Bros. T. Webster. C. Holden, W. Hill, W. Holden. J. Hall and J. Per- kins.</p>
        <p>A tea and social were enjoyed afterwards, and the prizes were presented by the Vice-Grand Master, Bro. Chas. Holden, and a hearty vote of thanks was passed to the donors of the prizes and to the committee, for the able manner in which the main had been conducted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>ODDFJELLOIVS' HANDICAP.</head>
        <p>ODDFJELLOIVS' HANDICAP.</p>
        <p>Draw For This Afternoon's Games.</p>
        <p>The annual bowling handicap in c,onneetion with the "Constitution " Lodge 1.0.0. F., M.U., will take place this (Saturday) afternoon, by kind per- mission of Mr. Frank Starkey, King and Queen Hotel Bowling Green, Padgate-lane, commencing at 2.15 prompt.</p>
        <p>First Round. Thomas E. James v. A. E. Burdett. Peter Lester v. Frank Lloyd. - John Woods (Mersey-street) v. J. H. James: A. Stacey v. A. Lawton. W. Warburton v. J. Ellison. W. Watson v. W. A. Warburton. Sam Wray v. M. Aukland. T. S. Steel v 2 J. W. Rigby. T. Ford v: John Mules. A. Wills v. F. Hambleton. N. G. Clarke v. W. Robinson. S. B. Mellor v. J. Woods (Fennel-street). H. Wilde v. Hy. Taylor. R. Kay v. Jas. Lawton. W. H. Ball v. Torn Woods. H. Kay v. A. Pennington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>TANNERS AT BOWLS.</head>
        <p>TANNERS AT BOWLS.</p>
        <p>At Gatacre on Saturday, the return fixture took place between Messrs. J. IL Fleming's Tannery team and the Northern Tanning Co., Ltd., Booth, the Warrington trundlers being successful by 42 Points. The day was gloriously fine, and a most enjoyable day was spent, the arrangements which the Bootle team had made with Mr. Wilson of the Bridge Inn, Gatacre, adding materially to the success of the day. The scores in the bowling match were:— Fennel-street Tannery. Northern Tanning Co. J. Melbourne J. Carter T. Allen J. Kirkam Alf. Savage H. McMahon G. Postlethwaite .. 21 S. Jones</p>
        <p>J. Higham L. Duane Arthur Savage .T: Proudman J. Boyle, jun. ..... 21 B. Jeffers S. Hardman ....... 21 N. Williams .. W. Boyle</p>
        <p>255 213 Majority for Fennel-street Tannery, 42 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>TENNIS.</head>
        <p>TENNIS.</p>
        <p>ATTRACTIONS OF THE GRAPPENHALL TENNIS GROUNDS.</p>
        <p>in the local world of tennis the Grappenhall Club holds an enviable position, having, from unpre- tentious beginnings, developed into an unqualified social success, thus realising the ambition of its founders.</p>
        <p>It was in splendid swing when the war broke out, but during the past year has suffered in per- haps-a larger ratio to its members than other clubs through the requirements of the country for men. of military age. No fewer than fifteen have gone out, and the seven male. members left behind are either ineligible for the Army or engaged on munition work. The membership now numbers twenty-seven ladies and seven gentlemen—a notable difference to a roll of fifty names, in which the sexes wire equally represented. Of the four courts at the Latchford Hockey Ground, the club's head- quarters, two have been closed temporarily. As in the case of other clubs, Grappenhall owes a great deal to the zeal and activity of its lady members. It has still another distinction: its originator was a lady, Mrs. Kerfoot, who now holds the honoured position of president of the ladies' committee. The club was originally formed as a private under- taking by some residents'in and 'about Legit Villas •to promote social intercourse, and, at an inaugural meeting on May 27th, 1908, Mr. A. Evans was elected chairman, Mr. Pharaoh, treasurer; and Mr. Wieks, secretary. During that season play took place cm Mr. Crompton's lawn, Victoria- avenue; which was placed free of charge at the disposal of the Joembers,, then fourteen in num- ber. Owing to increased membership the habitat was changed the following year to ground in Albert-avenue, where two courts were rented. Accommodation Doubled.</p>
        <p>Further expansion, however, became necessary in 1913, the club having grown so much in numbers and popularity that the accommodation had to be doubled: thus we find it in its present quarters. A broad expanse of sward in leafy seclusion and a roomy pavilion and tea room make the surround- ings most attractive, and many enjoyable Saturday afternoons have been spent at tea. talk and tennis. Of the original founders only two remain, Mrs. Kerfoot and Miss Jewkei. Mr. Joseph Carter having left the club; but to these three and to Mr. W. H. Hartley. who succeeded Mr. Carter as president in 1910, may be attributed the prosperous state of the G,rappenhall Lawn Tennis Club, all</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
