      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 15, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JULY 15, 1916.</p>
        <p>Education.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>!STAGE AND KINEMA.</head>
        <p>!STAGE AND KINEMA.</p>
        <p>What Is Coming Next Week.</p>
        <p>Royal Court.</p>
        <p>Closing foi: its summer redecorations at the end of next week, the Royal Court Theatre is going to perorate with the well-known musical comedy revue, entitled " S'hush ! "—a trip to the Isle of Man. It is reputed to be a capital piece of entertainment. Miss Nora Kebble is the principal lady, and she is to be suppoited by a London chorus. Music will be a strong feature. The theatre, will reopen on August Bank Holiday. Hippodrome.</p>
        <p>At the Palace and Hippodrome, J. Alexander's Nine Empire Opera Singers will contribute a considerable share of the pickgratnme. Another favourite turn will be that by Ernie Ream with his "Baby Grand "—that is to say, a F•rnall Piano. Others announced include: Tom Gott, comedian; Bryant and Biyant, novelty manipulators; and the t.,Larlie Meteor Trio, flying wonders. The Kinemas.</p>
        <p>At present at the Star Kinematthat great spectacular film, "An American's Home," is being shown. During the first half of next week the principal picture will be "Jimmy," by John Strange Winter. A first-class picture is the British' official "Destruction' of a Fokker." At the end of the week Mau Pick- ford will appear in "Poor Little P.ppiao," the film all London is talking about. "The House of Tears" is the main, picture now on view at the Grand Electric, Bridge Foot. During the first half of next week the seventh episode of "The Diamond from the Sky" and "Black Fear" will be ,screened, the "star" picture at the end of the week being "A Yellow Streak,"</p>
        <p>At the Pavilion, Lovely-lane, "Liberty Hall" now holds the fort, and during the first part of next week its place will be taken by ",Souls Aflame," "The Fox and the Pig,". the seventh episode of "The Diamond from the Sky." • During the secoml half of the week "The Colonel .from the Red Hussars, and 'Fatty's Bull Pup" will consumf most of the time. _</p>
        <p>The Picture House, Winmarleigh-street, are now showing an excellent film, entitled, ".The Kidnapped Stockbroker," and next week the main pictures for the first and second parts will be "The Family Secret" and "For Profes- sional Reasons."</p>
        <p>Greed" still holds sway at the Central, Sankey-street, and it will be continued during the second portion of next week. The series idea is proving a great success, and the fifth episode of "The Diamond from the Sky" will be shown on Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>BANK DIVIDENDS.</head>
        <p>BANK DIVIDENDS.</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE AND YORKSHIRE.</p>
        <p>The Directors of the Lancashire and York- shire Bank, Ltd., have declared an interim dividend for the past half-year of 16s. per share, subject to deduction of income tax. The same rate of dividend was paid last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>PAR R'S.</head>
        <p>PAR R'S.</p>
        <p>The Directors of Parr's Bank, Ltd., have •-laied an interim dividend for the half-year ended 30th June, 1916, at the rate of 18 per cent. per annum, subject to deduction of in- come tax, payable on and after sth August. This is at the same rate as the dividend for the half-year ended 31st December, 1915. LONDON CITY AND MIDLAND.</p>
        <p>The Directors of the London City and Mid- land Bank, Limited, announce an interini divi- dend for the past half-year at the rate of 18 per cent. per annUm, less income tax, payable on the 15th inst. Deposits amount to £157,539,135. Cash in hand, and at Bank of England, .238,888,014.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>UNION BANK.</head>
        <p>UNION BANK.</p>
        <p>The net profits of The Union Bank of Man- chester, Limited, for the half-year ended June 30th, 1916, including £25,919 brought forward, amount to ..t9,9.10. The Directors have de- clared a dividend of 124.- per cent., free of in- come tax, absorbing £34,375, payable on the 17th inst.; have provided £13,500 for income tax; &lt;25,000 for premises reduction; and £5,000 for pensions fund; carrying forward £42,035.</p>
        <p>MANCHESTER AND LIVERPOOL DISTRICT, The Directors of the Manchester and Liver- pool District Banking Co., Ltd., report -that, after making provision for rebate on bills, and for bad and doubtful debts, the gross profits for the half-year ended June 30th was .2328,350. The expenses amounted 'to .2137,149, leaving a net profit of .2191,201 for the half-year, to which must be added .2114,676 brought forward. The directors recommend the declaration of a divi- dend at the rate of m per cent. per annum, subject to the deduction of income tax. This will absorb .2165,900, leaving a balance of .2139,976 to carry forward.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>MR. HORACE BURNAGE'S SERIOUS</head>
        <p>MR. HORACE BURNAGE'S SERIOUS</p>
        <p>ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>We regret to state that Mr. Horace Burnage, assistant surveyor to the Warrington Rural District Council, sustained a severa accident last evening, both .of his le gs being broken be- low the knee. Mr. Manage was on his mbtor cycle proceeding from polborne-freet into Sankey-street, when he collided with a motor lorry travelling from .Liverpool. He was taken to the Infirmary án g on inquirz, a few hou'rs later, we were informed that he was "comfortable." Mr. Burnage's residence is London-road, Stockton Heath.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>LAY PREACHER'S APPEAL.</head>
        <p>LAY PREACHER'S APPEAL.</p>
        <p>One who conducts services at the Working Men's Mission, Bank Quay, Alfred Walker, 98, Liverpool-road, Sankey Bridges, went to the Appeal. Tribunal at Liverpool this week and asked for the refusal of the Warrington Rural District Tribunal to be reversed.—The appeal was disallowed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>Ex-Deputy-Registrar.</head>
        <p>Ex-Deputy-Registrar.</p>
        <p>- - The military representative appealed against the conditional exemption of one who had held the position of Deputy-Regi4rar at Warrington and had resigned in order to work on the farm belonging to his father, who is a prominent citizen residing at the Vulcan.—The appeal was allowed. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>Petition for Prohibition.</head>
        <p>Petition for Prohibition.</p>
        <p>Believing that the Great Offensive can only be maintained by means of an uninterrupted supply of munitions, and that the greatest hindrance to that supply is intoxicating drink, a movement has been set afoot for the prohibi- tion of its manufacture and sale during the war and for six months afterwards, almost identically with the action which has met with so much success in Russia. A phase of the campaign to athieve this object is the signing of a petition which will be plated ready for signature in the churches of Warrington to- morrow • (Sunday') and the following Sabbath Day. From most pulpits the petition will be mentioned, and the Rev. H. , Ogden, of Go!. borne-street Baptist Church, informs us that he purposes devoting his sermon entirely to the consideration of so great a problem. He is an ardent iiipporter of the movement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>CONSCIENCES.</head>
        <p>CONSCIENCES.</p>
        <p>Christadelphians Successful Appeal.</p>
        <p>Several cases of general interest were dealt with by the Warrington Tribunal at their sitting on Tuesday morning. John Helsby, aged 27, appealed for exemption as a conscientious objector and also on business grounds. He asked for his case to be heard in private and the Court was cleared. On resuming, the Chairman (Dr. Joseph, deputy-mayor). said that on the personal Claim the- Tribunal were of the opinion that his convictions were iather political than real conscientious objections under the Act. They quite believed in the sincerity of what he stated, but at the same time they did not consider the grounds anything but political. The personal appeal Would, therefore, be re- fused. On the business appal he would be granted exemption to tile eLat or the mouth; Christadelphians Exempted. The Tribunal -heard appeals from six mem- bers of the Christadelphian,Society, who asked for exemption as conscientious objectors. One applicant had been a member of the body re- ferred to for eleven years, while another had joined on May 21st, 1914—as .Alderman Tinnion remarked, "A narrow squeak:' The appli- cants were James Sutton, 28, wire weaver; Harry Fairbrother, 27, gas fitter, L. and N.W. R.; Thomas Cotterill, 27, oil filler; James Whittingham, 18, analyst; James Oakes, jun., 26, wire weaver; and •Harry Wareing, 28,, cabinetinaker. All had handed in written replies to the schedule of questions. The Chairman said the decision of the Tribunal was that they would ail be exempted from combatant service, and that they would get conditional exemption from non-combatant service, the condition being that they did some work of national importance. The Tribunal considered that all of them, with the exception of Wareing, were already so engaged.' If they left their present employment they Would have to get work of national importance to the satis- faction of the Tribunal, and with reglrd to Wareing, the application must be brought with- in 21 days showing' that he had taken up-work of national importance. Tannery Directors' Indispensability.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. Hatton (Messrs. llobert Davies and Co., solicitors), appeared on behalf of a firm of tanners, who sought Extmption for two of their directors, aged 38 and Mr. liallaway, clerk to the Tribunal, in- formed Mr. Hatton that he might, confine ,his statement to the younger one.- Mr. Hatton said there was no need to enlarge on the recent order. issued by the Goverament by which they commandeered all heavy leather. These were two directing heeds necessary for this business, and he thought he could also bring them within the certified occupations. The output was from 800 to 1,000 hides, repre- salting a turnover of £4,000 a week, or £200,000 per annum. The only mangers were the two in respect of which the application was made. The younger one had to superintend the inspec- tion, weighing and output of all the leather that went Out, he acted as -Keretary to the business, saw to the buying and saling. and looked _lifter the maintenance of the plant. In addition to that,- he did manual work such as rounding, hides, pumping, &amp;c. Applicant was 34 years of age, married, and with two children. The out- put of leather was practically all sold to the Government up to the end' of September, and the output could. not be maintained if these managers were taken. As a matter of fact, badges had been given to the men, and they were entitled to keep the men., It was really more indispensable to keep applicant. There were 23,437 actual orders, and commitments for Government leather to the 4th Sept mber amounting to 20 003. They had an order com- mandeering heavy leather of every kind. In reply to Mr. Hallaway, the Chairman of the Board of/ Directors said that. 43 men, nine boys and two girls were employed. There Was only one foreman. • Applicants were the two other directors. The business was three times as,great as it was a few years ago. - The Chairman asked if badges for the .Direc- tors had been applied for. The Chairman of Directors said the names of directors had been crossed off when the appli- cation was sent in.</p>
        <p>Mr. Halfway said he saw two directors walk- ing about with badges. The Chairman of Directors said if they had had their ,names put down as managers they would have received them right away, but they put down their names as directors and they were crossed off.</p>
        <p>The Tribunal granted the older applicant conditional exemption and the younger one three months' exemption. Versatile Society Secretary.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. S. Steel, solicitor, explained the posi- tion of the District Secretary of the Warriligton District of the Order of Ancint Shepherck, which numbers some. SOO cr 1 stt'io itieTnberi There are nine lodges in the Wqrriagton dis- trict, o,ne being at Lymra and alif,ther at Appleton. He was responsible for the 'v.-)!e district, and had to make out all the 'ret,irtly, He was at present engaged on the quinquennial return, which was quite an elaborate mass of figures and details. He was also secretary of the Morning Star branch of 180 members and of the Past Masters' Lodge,. formed with the object of starting and developing a thrift section. He also supervised the ,sick claims both on the voluntary and State sides, and saw to the dis- posal of the special levy made for the purpose of maintaining ,the contributions of all members who had gone to the war. There was further sick pay for discharged sick soldiers. A hundred "and forty members had joined his Majesty's Forces. The applicant also held the Offices of treasurer of the Warrington Friendly Societies Medical Association and secretary of the juvenile branch of the Shepherds Society, which comprised 27 members. He had other activities which with the foregoing were ex- clusive of his ordinary occupation in a War- rington firm, where he fulfilled the duties of cashier. . The Tribunal reserved their decision. Other Cases.</p>
        <p>A, fruiterer mild wagonette proprietor, for whom Mr. W. B. Forshaw appeared, 'Was granted conditional exemption. His age is 33, and he is a married man with three children. He• had six horses, three spring carts, three wagonettes fi n d a lorry to look after, and only employed two boys under 18 years of age. Messrs. Bradley's, clothiers, Bridge-street, sought conditional -exemption for the Manager of this branch of their business. Two assistants had joined • the Army from the Warrington house, and the manager _ in question was the</p>
        <p>_ only an left. The application was granted. The Tribunal allowed conditional exemption to a butcher who has contracts for meat sup- plies to various .homes and institutions, the ,latter including hospitals where wounded soldiers are under treatment. .</p>
        <p>_ Another firm of butchers applied for exemp- tion in the case of two employes. One was exempted conditionally and the other given until the end of October. A chemical 'plumber and a wire gauge worker, both engaged on Government _sub-con- tract§, were granted three months' exemption. Conditional exemption was the order made on the applications of a Jewish glazier and of two brothers engaged in coach repairing and smith work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>VOLUNTEER ORDERS.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEER ORDERS.</p>
        <p>Warrington Company.</p>
        <p>ORDERS FOR WEEK ENDING JULY 22nd, by W. PETER RYLANDS. Officer Commanding. Parades. -</p>
        <p>Monday and Thursday, 8 Pam, Bank Park. p.m., HankeY's Farm,,Wash; Saturday next, 3 lane.—Composite Company and Group B Re- serves. Uniform. Fire Brigade Drill.-7-Thursday, q. 45 p.m., Fire Station. Orderlies for the Week.—Sergeant Davies and Coiporal Blackwell.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>Group B Reserve.</head>
        <p>Group B Reserve.</p>
        <p>- - Nos. I and 2 Scoads•—Monday and Thursday, 8.p.m., Rifle Exercise&amp; No. 3 Squad.—Squad Drill, with arms. Class Firing.—Tuesday, from 7.30 p.m.; Sergt. Delooze in .charge. Saturday next, 3 p.m., Wash-lane, as detailed above.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>NEED FOR CARE.</head>
        <p>NEED FOR CARE.</p>
        <p>Suggestions Which Misled The</p>
        <p>Tribunal.</p>
        <p>Alleging that representations made in sup- port of a claim-were not strictly in accordance with fact," Captain Mathams had a builder summoned to Thursday's sitting of the Tribunal at the Town Hall.</p>
        <p>After reading the above declaration out, the Clerk (Mr. A. T. Hallaway) observed: "That is the Scottish method of saying they were untrue." _ _</p>
        <p>The Builder: A very nice way of putting it! Captain Mathams then explained that when the builder was before the Tribunal and given conditional exemption a short time ago he said he had lost the services of a man to whom he was paying more than the standard rate of wages, adding that he had gone into a munitions works in order to get out of going into the Army. He (Captain Mathams) now understood that the man denied that he was averse to going into the Army and that the reason he left the builder's employment was because he could earn more money elsewhere. The Builder: The man absolutely dreads going into the Army. He has told me that he could not .sleep at nights because of it. If he is willing to do his bit why should he bother? Mr. Bradbury (a member of the Tribunal) : The reason he was upset was because he thought you had tried to scandalise him by saying he was averse to military service. The Builder: The man said he would get out of the Army by going into the munition works. The Chairman (I)r. Joseph) : Th 4 Tribunal 'quite agree With the military representative that certain- suggestions were made by you which misled them. We were under the im- pression that the man went away from you in order to get out of going into the Army, though that had nothing to do with our decision. Yet any false impression might mislead us and we are glad the matter has been raised. The Clerk: This is a most serious matter, because you are liable to prosecution and im- prisonment. The Builder: I had no intention to mislead; you.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>Would Work Rather Than Fight.</head>
        <p>Would Work Rather Than Fight.</p>
        <p>A window cleaner, who appealed on domestic grounds, was asked whether he was not a con- scientious objector, and he replied in the negative.</p>
        <p>The Clerk: But you say you object to fight- ing? _ _</p>
        <p>Applicant: I object to fighting to keep my home. I would rather work, to keep it, because the money the Army would pay me would not be sufficient. My wife is of a nervous disposi- tion, and I should have to get a lady to keep her company if I left. I lost ,one business in Wigan; owing to the war, and my home was broken up. Now I have got another home to- gether in, Warrington, and that may have to be broken up new. I have lost all nay savings. Replying to • Captain Mathams, applicant: stated that the fish and fruit trade, in which he was engaged in Wigan, had been badly hit by the war. Captain Mathams : Is that, because so many people in Wigan are prepared to go and fight for their homes?</p>
        <p>Applicant It is immaterial to me whether I fight or not, so long as my wife is comfortable, and I know it.</p>
        <p>The Deputy-Mayor: The Tribunal have every reason to think your wife will be comfortably looked after if you have to go. The appeal is refused.</p>
        <p>Unattested Men Not Bodged.</p>
        <p>On a firm' of tanners appealing for the exemp- tion of two married men, aged 27 years, the Clerk asked: Why have they not been ba7dged?" The Representative of the Firm: Because they are not attested men. At the outset of Lord Derby's scheme we only badged the men who agreed to attest. There were just these two men who did not care to attest, so we did not badge them. _</p>
        <p>The Clerk As a sort of penalty? The Representative: No; but we did not want to put anybody behind a badge. Mr. Bradbury: What prompted the firm to make an exception of these men? The Representative replied that they did not want any shirkers behind the badge. They had badges to distribute from the Ministry of Munitions, and they did not want to give them to Tom, Diek and 'Harry if they were not agree- able to stay at their, work or go into the Army, as the case might be. Mr. Bradbury: What change has taken place thn,t \has caused you to appeal for them?—They are two men we cannot very well spare at the moment.</p>
        <p>Particulars in support were then given, bid the Tribunal refused the appeals. Mr. Harry Mather's Unsuccessful Appeal, Through Mr. T. S. Steel, Harry Mather, butcher, applied' for his slaughterman. The Chairman: You are a butcher yourself? —Yes.</p>
        <p>Could you do the slaughtering yourself?--I don't think so. I haven't done any for twenty years.</p>
        <p>Captain Mathams: But you can do it?—lt would want some going over. Application refused. Solicitors' Clerks.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. S. Steel appealed on behalf of his two clerks, married, stating that before the war he had six clerks; three of whom had joined the Forces, and one had left on account of ill- health. One of his clerks had been promoted to the rank of captain on the field of battle. The Chairman: We will postpone our decision for a fortnight. There are other analogous cases to yours, Mr. Steel, which we have to consider and we want to be coftsistent.</p>
        <p>Money Lender's Claim.</p>
        <p>In applying for the exemption of the manager of a Warrington and St. Helens branch of a money lending business, Mr. Arthur Browne, solicitor, said that if .the man went into the Army it was most likely that the debtors would repudiate their obligations._</p>
        <p>. Captain Mathams : This xs an appalling sug- gestion. Mr. Browne: I have had many years' experi- ence in County Courts and I know that unless ,you have 'someone to prove debt you will not get people to admit it. The application was refused on business grounds, but he was exempted until the end of August for domestic reasons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>CHESHIRE SIDE.</head>
        <p>THE CHESHIRE SIDE.</p>
        <p>Promised To Join V.T.C., But</p>
        <p>Did Not.</p>
        <p>There were 81 applications listed for hearing at the Runcorn Rural District Tribunal sitting in Dutton Workhouse on Thursday. 'Colonel Pilkington (military representative) said that very few of the men who had been required to join their local branches of the V.T.C. had cone .up yet. \Mr. Britch said that was his experience in his part of the county. The Clerk (Mr. W. E. Rough) said be had supplied a list to the secretaries of these Training Corps, and if the lists were returned showing men who had not joined he would 'notify them that unless they joined up at once they would have to attend the Tribunal to show cause why exemption should not be with- drawn A Sacrifice For Conscience.</p>
        <p>Joseph T. Bower, farm labourer, Stockton Heath, appealed on conscientious grounds. He is g 2 years of age, married, and a member of the Baptist Church. As a proof of making sacrifice for his principles, he said that before taking work as a farm labourer he held a posi- tion as accountant in a large firm. Sooner than attest he left the employment where he was twice as well paid as at present. Asked by the .Chairman (Mr. C. E. Linaker, sen.) if his convictions were so very strong that he would allow the Germans to walk in and rob and murder and plunder, he admitted that he would not lift his hand to defend his rela- tives. The Chairman: Then I 6= sorry for you. In reply to other questions he said that he could not help staying in the country, ,as no one was allowed to go out of it. The firm with whom he had been employed put up notices inducing men to attest, and when he refused to do so he was shifted about from one depart_ nint to another. In the end he left. The appeal was disallowed. William Clark, Stockton Heath, aged 40, a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>RAILWAY FARE ALLOWED</head>
        <p>RAILWAY FARE ALLOWED</p>
        <p>foreman joiner, also appealed on conscientious grounds, and stated that he was unwilling ti! undertake either 'combatant or noncombataul' service. Ti transpired that appellant is- el elder ministering in,the Warrington district 0, the., Association of Bible Students, and he gaff' forward the customary arguments in support o- lds, position. The New Testament teaching wae altogether opposed, to militarism, .he said. t The hearing was adjourned until the nel Tribunal. Still another conscientious objector appeared in the person of James T. Binder, a boiler inspector, living at Stockton Heath. He maxried Man, 37 years of age. He handed voluminous document, which took the Clerk minutes to read,. setting• out in the most al)" proved essayist Style the reasons why he "wouldn't." The Chairman pointed out that applicant inspected boilers ,in controlled works, and Ile, replied that most of these were not controllea before the war.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said the man who made 11 bullet *as as bad as the man that fired it, allru a' man who inspected boilers must come jay/ the same category. APplicant said his work was intended to sa" life and not to destroy it. He had held these convictions since he was 25, but did not prod&amp;e the testimony of-other persons to this effect. 1„e had resided in Stockton Heath from April, 191e' The -appeal was refused.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-07-15_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>PROCESSION AND PICNIC°</head>
        <p>PROCESSION AND PICNIC°</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Children Have Happy Day.</p>
        <p>The children—and a number of grown-up as well—of St. Thomas Parish had a splendid tin° yesterday, the weather being remarkably fine- short serVice was held in the Parish Church a 1.45, the Rev. Jas. Collins (vicar) and the Fes.' E. W. Clarke: officiating. Half an hour later ° procession, quite a lengthy one, was formed oy Mr. Fred Rose (choirmaster), who acted a' marshal, and ,the following were also present; Messrs. James Richardson (churchwarden), Hallows, F. Berry, Dowling and J. Swinte° (sideimen). General regret was felt at s 'absende of Mr. William Mclntyre, who away visiting his wounded son in a London 11°5- pital. First came the Church banner, borne by luelß' • hers of the Men's Bible Class, Messrs. Morrie, Wheeler, Smith and Pettey, followed by tlig Bluecoat School Band. Next in order marche'; the Women's and Girls' Bible Classes, younger girls,, all chariaingly dressed, tn, buglers and drummers of the Boy Scouts, till; Scouts' contingent, and the boys of the Pnrisi.o School. Bannerettes and small flags carried in the procession, which wound up several lorries and a couple of pony traps lade° with very small children, all in the highest glee, and good humour. They were. seated in tier"' on the Waggons, but appeared amazingly haPP/' nevertheless. to The procession passed over the swing bridge the confines of , the parish -on the Warringo.; side—the bridge was good-natured on occasion—and then moving to the ,boundarY Walton: returned via London-road. Tea Ivai, served by the district .visitors' in the Chtllci, Room and the Parish School, after which gathering reassembled in the Brewery Par •if where sports and pastimes concluded a cer' happy day. The ladies assisting were Mrs. Woods, teacher'. Women's Bible Class; Mrs. Worrell, Junior Bible Class; and the Misses AllensfT, A.stall, Berry, Hackett. Garnett, Rose. Redchso't Moore and Woods. Vehicles were kindly liettr, by Messrs. Greon,ll, Whitley and Co., Hughes, Grappenhall-road; Mrs. Morris an° '— Parton-</p>
      </div>
