        <p>PRIVATE H. SUTCLIFFE. Reported Dead.</p>
        <p>Sons, Ltd., enlisted in August, 1914, and came safely through the heavy fighting of June 16th last year. He was educated at Holy Trinity School, and was a chorister at St. Luke's Church, to which Bible Class he also belonged. He was formerly a member of the Church Lads' Brigade and Boy Scouts.</p>
        <p>WOUNDED. PRIVATE B. BORROWS. A \llylands's Employe Shot Through The Body. Private Benjamin Borrows, of the Devon- shire itegiment, son of Mr. William Borrows, 88, Evelyn-street, was severely wounded on June 19th, when a bullet entered his back and puBsed right through his body. The sister-in- charge of the field hosPital to which the -gal- lant soldier was taken; and the Chaplain, wrote to his father and mother with the request that they should not "worry" about him. A letter, dated July Ist, from the' sister-in-charge of a General Hospital in France, to 'which he was moved, states that the patient is ,going on as well as can be expected and hoped .to be allowed to come to an English hospital soon. Private Borrows, who enlisted in 1914, is a Eylands-_ Bros.' employe, and an old Sankey School scholar. When on furlough a few weeks</p>
