        <p>came through unscathed. He is the father of 'four children. PRIVATE A. C. 110 ROBIN. • In The Company That Was First Over The Trenches. Private A. Cooper Horobin, 51, Sankey-street, of the King's (Liverpool) Regiment, was wounded in the head on July let. In a very cheery letter from a Base Hospital, 'somewhere in France,' he writes : "My company was first over the parapet, being greeted by a terrible fire, mostly machine gun and artillery, though a few Boches stuck to their posts and sniped till we were on them. It was up to us for the German second trench,</p>
        <p>• • • . • , , *•-• . . •-• . • •</p>
        <p>with which I got hit went clean through the . leg without hitting the bone, and the doctor says it was a miracle." Private Clarke, who is 19 years of age, has been in the Army about twelve months, and has been at the Front since January of this year. He was formerly elflpl4)3-ed at Orford Tannery, and was educated at the. National and. Beamont , Schools. Another brother, Ernest, is in. training with a Welsh Regiment. LANCE-C9RPL. J. H: WARBURTON. Wounded In The Lip By A • German Bomb. Though the medium of a letter thrown through the window of a hospital train as 'it</p>
