        <p>./ • `LANCE-CORPORAL HENRY WARBURTO Wounded.</p>
        <p>passed through Bank Quay Station, Mrs. War- burton, a widow, 45, Fairclough's-avenue, re-' cqived news that her son, Lance-Corporal J. H. Warburton, of the Rifle Brigade, had been wounded in the lip by a German bomb, but not seriously. Lance-Corporal Warburton, whose father lost his life in a boating accident some years ago, is in his 21st year. He enlisted soon after the outbreak of war and has been in France nearly twelve months. He was formerly 6mployed as a range fitter by the Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Company. Whilst at the Front he was employed as a machine gunner, brigade scout, and latterly bomb-thrower. RIFLEMAN 11. NURSE. • Weklinown Singer Receives His' • Second Wound. For the second time Rifleman Harry Nurse, of the King's Liverpool Rifles, has been wounded, but he has written to his parents from a hospital in France stating that it is not serious and he does not expect to be drafted to England. A piece of shrapnel</p>
